[[:db/cas 17592186073455 :question/problem "Complete the ratio table below.\n\n| $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}?}$ | $10$ |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}?}$ | $35$ |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}?}$ | $50$ |" "Complete the ratio table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}?}$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}?}$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$35$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}?}$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$50$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186073455 :question/worked-solution "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}3}:{\\color{red}5}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}5}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry (${\\color{blue}3}$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry (${\\color{red}5}$).\n\nWe can generate equivalent ratios by finding equivalent fractions:\n\n- To create a denominator of $10,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}\\times 2}{{\\color{red}5}\\times 2} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}6}}{\\color{red}10}\n$$\n\n- To create a denominator of $35,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $7{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}\\times 7}{{\\color{red}5}\\times 7} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}21}}{\\color{red}35}\n$$\n\n- To create a denominator of $50,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $10{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}\\times 10}{{\\color{red}5}\\times 10} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}30}}{\\color{red}50}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n| ${\\color{blue}3}$ | ${\\color{red}5}$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}6}$ | ${\\color{red}10}$ |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}21}$ | ${\\color{red}35}$ |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}30}$ | ${\\color{red}50}$ |" "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}3}:{\\color{red}5}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}5}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry (${\\color{blue}3}$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry (${\\color{red}5}$).\n\nWe can generate equivalent ratios by finding equivalent fractions:\n\n- To create a denominator of $10,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}\\times 2}{{\\color{red}5}\\times 2} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}6}}{\\color{red}10}\n$$\n\n- To create a denominator of $35,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $7{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}\\times 7}{{\\color{red}5}\\times 7} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}21}}{\\color{red}35}\n$$\n\n- To create a denominator of $50,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $10{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}\\times 10}{{\\color{red}5}\\times 10} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}30}}{\\color{red}50}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}5}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}6}$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}10}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}21}$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}35}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}30}$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}50}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186073456 :question/problem "Harvey's parents reward him for each hour he spends helping with the housework. The following ratio table shows the number of hours of work Harvey did over the last three weeks and the corresponding rewards.\n\n| Reward (in dollars) | Hours of work |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}?}$ | $3$ |\n| $12$ | $4$ |\n| $15$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}?}$ |\n\nFrom top to bottom, what are the missing numbers in the ratio table?" "Harvey's parents reward him for each hour he spends helping with the housework. The following ratio table shows the number of hours of work Harvey did over the last three weeks and the corresponding rewards.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reward (in dollars)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Hours of work</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}?}$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}?}$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFrom top to bottom, what are the missing numbers in the ratio table?"] [:db/cas 17592186073456 :question/worked-solution "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}12}:{\\color{red}4}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}12}}{{\\color{red}4}}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow we divide the numerator and the denominator by $4$ to find an equivalent ratio:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{12}}\\div 4}{{\\color{red}{4}}\\div 4} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}}{{\\color{red}1}}\n$$\n\nWe can generate ratios that are equivalent to $3:1$ by finding more equivalent fractions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\times 3}{1\\times 3} & =\\frac{9}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3\\times 5}{1\\times 5} & =\\frac{15}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n| Reward (in dollars) | Hours of work |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}9}$ | $3$ |\n| $12$ | $4$ |\n| $15$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}5}$ |\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the missing numbers are $9$ and $5.$" "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}12}:{\\color{red}4}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}12}}{{\\color{red}4}}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow we divide the numerator and the denominator by $4$ to find an equivalent ratio:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{12}}\\div 4}{{\\color{red}{4}}\\div 4} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}}{{\\color{red}1}}\n$$\n\nWe can generate ratios that are equivalent to $3:1$ by finding more equivalent fractions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\times 3}{1\\times 3} & =\\frac{9}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3\\times 5}{1\\times 5} & =\\frac{15}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reward (in dollars)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Hours of work</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}9}$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}5}$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the missing numbers are $9$ and $5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186073514 :question/problem "![](images/bd/bdb8f7a69cc9270c2c69929af3392b5587768748fb437604b6696135f516aca1.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $\\overline{AH} \\perp \\overline{BC}$, and $\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$.\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> *The angles $\\angle {B}$ and $\\angle {C}$ are congruent.*\n\nThe table below gives the proof of the statement. Select the correct options in the table and thus complete the proof.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\angle{AHB}$ and $\\angle{AHC}$ are right angles |  |\n| 2 | $\\triangle{AHB}$ and $\\triangle{AHC}$ are right triangles |  |\n| 3 | $\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$ |  |\n| 4 | $\\overline{AH} \\cong \\overline{AH}$ |  |\n| 5 | $\\triangle{AHB} \\cong \\triangle{AHC}$ |  |\n| 6 | $\\angle B \\cong \\angle C$ |  |" "![](images/bd/bdb8f7a69cc9270c2c69929af3392b5587768748fb437604b6696135f516aca1.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $\\overline{AH} \\perp \\overline{BC}$, and $\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$.\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> *The angles $\\angle {B}$ and $\\angle {C}$ are congruent.*\n\nThe table below gives the proof of the statement. Select the correct options in the table and thus complete the proof.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$\\angle{AHB}$ and $\\angle{AHC}$ are right angles</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{AHB}$ and $\\triangle{AHC}$ are right triangles</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AH} \\cong \\overline{AH}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{AHB} \\cong \\triangle{AHC}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$\\angle B \\cong \\angle C$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186073514 :question/worked-solution "|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\angle{AHB}$ and $\\angle{AHC}$ are right angles | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\overline{AH} \\perp \\overline{BC}}$ |\n| 2 | $\\triangle{AHB}$ and $\\triangle{AHC}$ are right triangles | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of a right triangle}}$ |\n| 3 | $\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Given}}$ |\n| 4 | $\\overline{AH} \\cong \\overline{AH}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Reflexive property of congruence}}$ |\n| 5 | $\\triangle{AHB} \\cong \\triangle{AHC}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{HL criterion}}$ |\n| 6 | $\\angle B \\cong \\angle C$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{CPCTC}}$ |\n\nLet's examine each row in turn.\n\n1. We are given that $\\overline{AH} \\perp \\overline{BC}$. Therefore, $\\angle{AHB}$ and $\\angle{AHC}$ are right angles by the definition of perpendicular lines.\n2. By the definition of a right triangle, we have from row 1 that $\\triangle{AHB}$ and $\\triangle{AHC}$ are right triangles.\n3. We are given that $\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$ in the question stem.\n4. The reflexive property of congruence states that every segment is congruent to itself. Therefore, $\\overline{AH} \\cong \\overline{AH}$.\n5. From rows 2, 3, and 4, we have that $\\triangle{ABH}$ and $\\triangle{ACH}$ are right triangles, and\n$\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC}, \\qquad \\overline{AH} \\cong \\overline{AH}$.\nTherefore, $\\triangle{AHB} \\cong \\triangle{AHC}$ by the HL criterion.\n6. From row 5, we have $\\triangle AH{\\color{blue}B} \\cong \\triangle AH{\\color{blue}C}$. Therefore, $\\angle {\\color{blue}B} \\cong \\angle {\\color{blue}C}$ by CPCTC." "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$\\angle{AHB}$ and $\\angle{AHC}$ are right angles</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\overline{AH} \\perp \\overline{BC}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{AHB}$ and $\\triangle{AHC}$ are right triangles</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of a right triangle}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Given}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AH} \\cong \\overline{AH}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Reflexive property of congruence}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{AHB} \\cong \\triangle{AHC}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{HL criterion}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$\\angle B \\cong \\angle C$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{CPCTC}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's examine each row in turn.\n\n1. We are given that $\\overline{AH} \\perp \\overline{BC}$. Therefore, $\\angle{AHB}$ and $\\angle{AHC}$ are right angles by the definition of perpendicular lines.\n2. By the definition of a right triangle, we have from row 1 that $\\triangle{AHB}$ and $\\triangle{AHC}$ are right triangles.\n3. We are given that $\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$ in the question stem.\n4. The reflexive property of congruence states that every segment is congruent to itself. Therefore, $\\overline{AH} \\cong \\overline{AH}$.\n5. From rows 2, 3, and 4, we have that $\\triangle{ABH}$ and $\\triangle{ACH}$ are right triangles, and\n$\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC}, \\qquad \\overline{AH} \\cong \\overline{AH}$.\nTherefore, $\\triangle{AHB} \\cong \\triangle{AHC}$ by the HL criterion.\n6. From row 5, we have $\\triangle AH{\\color{blue}B} \\cong \\triangle AH{\\color{blue}C}$. Therefore, $\\angle {\\color{blue}B} \\cong \\angle {\\color{blue}C}$ by CPCTC."] [:db/cas 17592186073515 :question/problem "![](images/82/820871c183af4d5455807ba599aad2f3efeecf3b77a98609434bcb3c1763696a.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{BC}$.\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> *The angles $\\angle {BAM}$ and $\\angle {CAM}$ are congruent.*\n\nThe table below gives the proof of the statement. Select the correct options in the table and thus complete the proof.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $BM=CM$ | Definition of a midpoint |\n| 2 | $\\overline{BM} \\cong \\overline{CM}$ |  |\n| 3 | $AB=6\\,\\textrm{cm}$ | Given |\n| 4 | $AC=6\\,\\textrm{cm}$ | Given |\n| 5 | $AB=AC$ |  |\n| 6 | $\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$ | Definition of congruent segments |\n| 7 | $\\overline{AM} \\cong \\overline{AM}$ |  |\n| 8 | $\\triangle ABM \\cong \\triangle ACM$ |  |\n| 9 | $\\angle {BAM}\\cong \\angle {CAM}$ | CPCTC |" "![](images/82/820871c183af4d5455807ba599aad2f3efeecf3b77a98609434bcb3c1763696a.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{BC}$.\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> *The angles $\\angle {BAM}$ and $\\angle {CAM}$ are congruent.*\n\nThe table below gives the proof of the statement. Select the correct options in the table and thus complete the proof.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$BM=CM$</td>\n<td>Definition of a midpoint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\overline{BM} \\cong \\overline{CM}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$AB=6\\,\\textrm{cm}$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$AC=6\\,\\textrm{cm}$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$AB=AC$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$</td>\n<td>Definition of congruent segments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AM} \\cong \\overline{AM}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">8</td>\n<td>$\\triangle ABM \\cong \\triangle ACM$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">9</td>\n<td>$\\angle {BAM}\\cong \\angle {CAM}$</td>\n<td>CPCTC</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186073515 :question/worked-solution "|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $BM=CM$ | Definition of a midpoint |\n| 2 | $\\overline{BM} \\cong \\overline{CM}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent segments}}$ |\n| 3 | $AB=6\\,\\textrm{cm}$ | Given |\n| 4 | $AC=6\\,\\textrm{cm}$ | Given |\n| 5 | $AB=AC$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Transitive property of equality}}$ |\n| 6 | $\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$ | Definition of congruent segments |\n| 7 | $\\overline{AM} \\cong \\overline{AM}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Reflexive property of congruence}}$ |\n| 8 | $\\triangle ABM \\cong \\triangle ACM$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{SSS criterion}}$ |\n| 9 | $\\angle {BAM}\\cong \\angle {CAM}$ | CPCTC |\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 2. From row 1, we have that $BM=CM$ Therefore, $\\overline{BM} \\cong \\overline{CM}$ by the definition of congruent segments.\n- Consider row 5. From rows 3 and 4, we have that $AB=6\\,\\textrm{cm}=AC$. Therefore, $AB=AC$ by the transitive property of equality.\n- Consider row 7. Since every segment is congruent with itself, by the reflexive property of congruence, $\\overline{AM} \\cong \\overline{AM}$.\n- Consider row 8. From rows 2, 6, and 7, we have\n$\\overline{BM} \\cong \\overline{CM}, \\qquad\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC}, \\qquad \\overline{AM} \\cong \\overline{AM}$.\nTherefore, $\\triangle ABM \\cong \\triangle ACM$ by the SSS criterion." "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$BM=CM$</td>\n<td>Definition of a midpoint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\overline{BM} \\cong \\overline{CM}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent segments}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$AB=6\\,\\textrm{cm}$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$AC=6\\,\\textrm{cm}$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$AB=AC$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Transitive property of equality}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$</td>\n<td>Definition of congruent segments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AM} \\cong \\overline{AM}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Reflexive property of congruence}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">8</td>\n<td>$\\triangle ABM \\cong \\triangle ACM$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{SSS criterion}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">9</td>\n<td>$\\angle {BAM}\\cong \\angle {CAM}$</td>\n<td>CPCTC</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 2. From row 1, we have that $BM=CM$ Therefore, $\\overline{BM} \\cong \\overline{CM}$ by the definition of congruent segments.\n- Consider row 5. From rows 3 and 4, we have that $AB=6\\,\\textrm{cm}=AC$. Therefore, $AB=AC$ by the transitive property of equality.\n- Consider row 7. Since every segment is congruent with itself, by the reflexive property of congruence, $\\overline{AM} \\cong \\overline{AM}$.\n- Consider row 8. From rows 2, 6, and 7, we have\n$\\overline{BM} \\cong \\overline{CM}, \\qquad\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC}, \\qquad \\overline{AM} \\cong \\overline{AM}$.\nTherefore, $\\triangle ABM \\cong \\triangle ACM$ by the SSS criterion."] [:db/cas 17592186073516 :question/problem "![](images/77/771d17ad57e198b36db6d632003a34ea56ab83b4daf2c489ca839f6a2fcff8e3.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the points $A$, $C$, and $D$ are colinear, and the lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD}$ are parallel.\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> $m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$\n\nThe table below gives the proof of the statement. Complete the proof by filling in the empty boxes.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD}$ |  |\n| 2 | $\\angle{BCD} \\cong \\angle{CBE}$ |  |\n| 3 | $m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{CBE}$ |  |\n| 4 | $m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{ABE} + m\\angle{ABC}$ |  |\n| 5 | $\\angle{ABE} \\cong \\angle{A}$ |  |\n| 6 | $m\\angle{ABE} = m\\angle{A}$ |  |\n| 7 | $m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$ |  |\n| 8 | $m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$ |  |" "![](images/77/771d17ad57e198b36db6d632003a34ea56ab83b4daf2c489ca839f6a2fcff8e3.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the points $A$, $C$, and $D$ are colinear, and the lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD}$ are parallel.\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> $m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$\n\nThe table below gives the proof of the statement. Complete the proof by filling in the empty boxes.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\angle{BCD} \\cong \\angle{CBE}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{CBE}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{ABE} + m\\angle{ABC}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$\\angle{ABE} \\cong \\angle{A}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{ABE} = m\\angle{A}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">8</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186073516 :question/worked-solution "![](images/00/008b75ac2feaec944a50368ec0fc5781d47314f8bfe0503ae70c1e564b4035b1.png)\n\nThe correct proof is below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Given}}$ |\n| 2 | $\\angle{BCD} \\cong \\angle{CBE}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Alternate interior angles theorem}}$ |\n| 3 | $m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{CBE}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$ |\n| 4 | $m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{ABE} + m\\angle{ABC}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Angle addition postulate}}$ |\n| 5 | $\\angle{ABE} \\cong \\angle{A}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Alternate interior angles theorem}}$ |\n| 6 | $m\\angle{ABE} = m\\angle{A}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$ |\n| 7 | $m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Substitution}}$ |\n| 8 | $m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Transitive property of equality}}$ |\n\nLet's examine each row in turn.\n\n1. Note that we're given $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD}$.\n2. From row 1, we have that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD}$ are parallel. Therefore, $\\angle{BCD}$ and $\\angle{CBE}$ are congruent by the alternate interior angles theorem.\n3. From row 2, we have that $\\angle{BCD}$ and $\\angle{CBE}$ are congruent. Therefore, by the definition of congruent angles, $m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{CBE}$.\n4. Note that the segment $\\overline{AB}$ divides the angle $\\angle{CBE}$ into consecutive angles $\\angle{ABE}$ and $\\angle{ABC}$. Therefore, by the angle addition postulate, we must have\n$m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{ABE} + m\\angle{ABC}$.\n5. From row 1, we have that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD}$ are parallel. Therefore, $\\angle{ABE}$ and $\\angle{A}$ are congruent by the alternate interior angles theorem.\n6. From row 5, $\\angle{ABE}$ and $\\angle{A}$ are congruent. Therefore, by the definition of congruent angles, $m\\angle{ABE} = m\\angle{A}$.\n7. From row 6, $m\\angle{ABE} = m\\angle{A}$. Substituting this into the equation in row 4, we have\n$m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$.\n8. From rows 3 and 7, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{CBE}, \\qquad m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}.\n$$\nTherefore, by the transitive property of equality, $m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$." "![](images/00/008b75ac2feaec944a50368ec0fc5781d47314f8bfe0503ae70c1e564b4035b1.png)\n\nThe correct proof is below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Given}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\angle{BCD} \\cong \\angle{CBE}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Alternate interior angles theorem}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{CBE}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{ABE} + m\\angle{ABC}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Angle addition postulate}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$\\angle{ABE} \\cong \\angle{A}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Alternate interior angles theorem}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{ABE} = m\\angle{A}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Substitution}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">8</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Transitive property of equality}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's examine each row in turn.\n\n1. Note that we're given $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD}$.\n2. From row 1, we have that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD}$ are parallel. Therefore, $\\angle{BCD}$ and $\\angle{CBE}$ are congruent by the alternate interior angles theorem.\n3. From row 2, we have that $\\angle{BCD}$ and $\\angle{CBE}$ are congruent. Therefore, by the definition of congruent angles, $m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{CBE}$.\n4. Note that the segment $\\overline{AB}$ divides the angle $\\angle{CBE}$ into consecutive angles $\\angle{ABE}$ and $\\angle{ABC}$. Therefore, by the angle addition postulate, we must have\n$m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{ABE} + m\\angle{ABC}$.\n5. From row 1, we have that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD}$ are parallel. Therefore, $\\angle{ABE}$ and $\\angle{A}$ are congruent by the alternate interior angles theorem.\n6. From row 5, $\\angle{ABE}$ and $\\angle{A}$ are congruent. Therefore, by the definition of congruent angles, $m\\angle{ABE} = m\\angle{A}$.\n7. From row 6, $m\\angle{ABE} = m\\angle{A}$. Substituting this into the equation in row 4, we have\n$m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$.\n8. From rows 3 and 7, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{CBE}, \\qquad m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}.\n$$\nTherefore, by the transitive property of equality, $m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$."] [:db/cas 17592186073520 :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e0fe9cf7b5a39827133e7ea4648d9aac946e4711cb877cf518bc11587d97712.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the points $B$, $C$, and $D$ are colinear.\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> $m\\angle{ACD} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{B}$\n\nThe table below provides proof of the statement. Complete the proof by filling in the empty boxes.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\angle{ACB}$ and $\\angle{ACD}$ are supplementary |  |\n| 2 | $m\\angle{ACB} + m\\angle{ACD} = 180^\\circ$ |  |\n| 3 | $m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{B} + m\\angle{ACB} = 180^\\circ$ | Interior angles of a triangle theorem |\n| 4 | $180^\\circ = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{B} + m\\angle{ACB}$ |  |\n| 5 | $m\\angle{ACB} + m\\angle{ACD} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{B} + m\\angle{ACB}$ | Transitive property of equality |\n| 6 | $m\\angle{ACD} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{B}$ | Addition principle |" "![](images/0e/0e0fe9cf7b5a39827133e7ea4648d9aac946e4711cb877cf518bc11587d97712.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the points $B$, $C$, and $D$ are colinear.\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> $m\\angle{ACD} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{B}$\n\nThe table below provides proof of the statement. Complete the proof by filling in the empty boxes.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$\\angle{ACB}$ and $\\angle{ACD}$ are supplementary</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{ACB} + m\\angle{ACD} = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{B} + m\\angle{ACB} = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Interior angles of a triangle theorem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$180^\\circ = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{B} + m\\angle{ACB}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{ACB} + m\\angle{ACD} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{B} + m\\angle{ACB}$</td>\n<td>Transitive property of equality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{ACD} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{B}$</td>\n<td>Addition principle</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186073520 :question/worked-solution "The complete proof is below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\angle{ACB}$ and $\\angle{ACD}$ are supplementary | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Linear pair postulate}}$ |\n| 2 | $m\\angle{ACB} + m\\angle{ACD} = 180^\\circ$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of supplementary angles}}$ |\n| 3 | $m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{B} + m\\angle{ACB} = 180^\\circ$ | Interior angles of a triangle theorem |\n| 4 | $180^\\circ = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{B} + m\\angle{ACB}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Symmetric property of equality}}$ |\n| 5 | $m\\angle{ACB} + m\\angle{ACD} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{B} + m\\angle{ACB}$ | Transitive property of equality |\n| 6 | $m\\angle{ACD} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{B}$ | Addition principle |\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 1. According to the linear pair postulate, $\\angle{ACB}$ and $\\angle{ACD}$ are supplementary angles.\n- Consider row 2. From row 1, $\\angle{ACB}$ and $\\angle{ACD}$ are supplementary angles. Therefore, by definition of supplementary angles,\n$m\\angle{ACB} + m\\angle{ACD} = 180^\\circ$.\n- Consider row 4. From row 3, we have\n$m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{B} + m\\angle{ACB} = 180^\\circ$.\nNow, using the symmetric property of equality, we get\n$180^\\circ = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{B} + m\\angle{ACB}$." "The complete proof is below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$\\angle{ACB}$ and $\\angle{ACD}$ are supplementary</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Linear pair postulate}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{ACB} + m\\angle{ACD} = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of supplementary angles}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{B} + m\\angle{ACB} = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Interior angles of a triangle theorem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$180^\\circ = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{B} + m\\angle{ACB}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Symmetric property of equality}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{ACB} + m\\angle{ACD} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{B} + m\\angle{ACB}$</td>\n<td>Transitive property of equality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{ACD} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{B}$</td>\n<td>Addition principle</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 1. According to the linear pair postulate, $\\angle{ACB}$ and $\\angle{ACD}$ are supplementary angles.\n- Consider row 2. From row 1, $\\angle{ACB}$ and $\\angle{ACD}$ are supplementary angles. Therefore, by definition of supplementary angles,\n$m\\angle{ACB} + m\\angle{ACD} = 180^\\circ$.\n- Consider row 4. From row 3, we have\n$m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{B} + m\\angle{ACB} = 180^\\circ$.\nNow, using the symmetric property of equality, we get\n$180^\\circ = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{B} + m\\angle{ACB}$."] [:db/cas 17592186073535 :question/worked-solution "To find out the number of boxes Brian's mother will need, we have to divide $192$ by $12$.\n\nWe write our division this way:\n\n|  |  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $12$ | $1$$9$$2$ |  |  |\n\nSince\n\n$$\n12 \\times {\\color{blue}10} = 120\n$$\n\nis less than $192$, we subt