one\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\fbox{0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe get $\\fbox{0}$, so the division is done.\n\nThe quotient is the sum of the numbers on top of the boxes:\n\n$$\n50+5+2=57\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n1,482 \\div 26 = 57\\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073565 :question/problem "By finding the missing values in the ratio table below, determine the graph that represents the given relationship.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $2$ |\n| $8$ |  |\n|  | $6$ |" "By finding the missing values in the ratio table below, determine the graph that represents the given relationship.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td><td data-fill=\"#C0C1C2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#C0C1C2\"></td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186073565 :question/worked-solution "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}4}:{\\color{red}2}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}4}}{{\\color{red}2}}\n$$\n\nWe can generate equivalent ratios by finding equivalent fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4\\times 2}{2\\times 2} & =\\frac{8}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4\\times 3}{2\\times 3} & =\\frac{12}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our ratio table:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $2$ |\n| $8$ | $4$ |\n| $12$ | $6$ |\n\nSo, we have the following ordered pairs: $(4,2),$ $(8,4),$ $(12,6).$\n\nPlotting these points on the coordinate plane and connecting them with a straight line, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/8d/8def1b37d62f91a16109db0da3287042fe5893b97bf447e6ee16946b3f8d4bfc.png)" "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}4}:{\\color{red}2}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}4}}{{\\color{red}2}}\n$$\n\nWe can generate equivalent ratios by finding equivalent fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4\\times 2}{2\\times 2} & =\\frac{8}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4\\times 3}{2\\times 3} & =\\frac{12}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our ratio table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#C0C1C2\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#C0C1C2\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, we have the following ordered pairs: $(4,2),$ $(8,4),$ $(12,6).$\n\nPlotting these points on the coordinate plane and connecting them with a straight line, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/8d/8def1b37d62f91a16109db0da3287042fe5893b97bf447e6ee16946b3f8d4bfc.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186073580 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we can pick out the following coordinates:\n$(2,1),\\: (4,2),\\: (6,3),\\: (8,4)$\n\nNow let's put these coordinates into the table:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | $1$ |\n| $4$ | $2$ |\n| $6$ | $3$ |\n| $8$ | $4$ |\n\nNotice that for every $x$-value, we divide by $2$ to get the corresponding $y$-value.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | $\\dfrac2{\\color{blue}2} = 1$ |\n| $4$ | $\\dfrac4{\\color{blue}2} = 2$ |\n| $6$ | $\\dfrac6{\\color{blue}2} = 3$ |\n| $8$ | $\\dfrac8{\\color{blue}2} = 4$ |\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{x}{\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\color{blue}2}}\n$$\n\nSo the missing number is ${\\color{blue}{2}}$." "From the graph, we can pick out the following coordinates:\n$(2,1),\\: (4,2),\\: (6,3),\\: (8,4)$\n\nNow let's put these coordinates into the table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNotice that for every $x$-value, we divide by $2$ to get the corresponding $y$-value.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac2{\\color{blue}2} = 1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac4{\\color{blue}2} = 2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac6{\\color{blue}2} = 3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac8{\\color{blue}2} = 4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{x}{\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\color{blue}2}}\n$$\n\nSo the missing number is ${\\color{blue}{2}}$."] [:db/cas 17592186073581 :question/worked-solution "First, we create a table representing the equation\n\n$$\ny = x + {\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nFor some chosen values of $x$, we evaluate $y{:}$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0+{\\color{blue}3} = 3$ |\n| $1$ | $1+{\\color{blue}3} = 4$ |\n| $2$ | $2+{\\color{blue}3} = 5$ |\n| $3$ | $3+{\\color{blue}3} = 6$ |\n| $4$ | $4+{\\color{blue}3} = 7$ |\n\nNow, we plot these points and connect them with a line. When we do this, we get the following plot.\n\n![](images/4b/4ba64522cfdee4412cddd08465390c256b2255d2a721d75a20e7e9357def4c00.png)" "First, we create a table representing the equation\n\n$$\ny = x + {\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nFor some chosen values of $x$, we evaluate $y{:}$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0+{\\color{blue}3} = 3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1+{\\color{blue}3} = 4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2+{\\color{blue}3} = 5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3+{\\color{blue}3} = 6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4+{\\color{blue}3} = 7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, we plot these points and connect them with a line. When we do this, we get the following plot.\n\n![](images/4b/4ba64522cfdee4412cddd08465390c256b2255d2a721d75a20e7e9357def4c00.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186073583 :question/worked-solution "Since the laptop charges for $23$ minutes, we have that $t=23$.\n\nOn the graph, we can't identify a point corresponding to $t=23$, so we will need to find the equation relating $p$ and $t$.\n\nFrom the graph, we can pick out the following coordinates:\n$(5,10),\\: (10,20),\\: (15,30)$\n\nNow, let's put these coordinates into a table:\n\n| $t$ | $p$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $5$ | $10$ |\n| $10$ | $20$ |\n| $15$ | $30$ |\n\nNotice that for every $t$-value, we multiply by $2$ to get the corresponding $p$-value.\n\n| $t$ | $p$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $5$ | ${\\color{blue}2}\\cdot5=10$ |\n| $10$ | ${\\color{blue}2}\\cdot10=20$ |\n| $15$ | ${\\color{blue}2}\\cdot15=30$ |\n\nHence, the equation that relates $p$ and $t$ is\n\n$$\np={\\color{blue}2}t\n$$\n\nSo, for $t=23$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= 2 \\cdot 23 = 46\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $23$ minutes, the charge level will be $46\\%$." "Since the laptop charges for $23$ minutes, we have that $t=23$.\n\nOn the graph, we can't identify a point corresponding to $t=23$, so we will need to find the equation relating $p$ and $t$.\n\nFrom the graph, we can pick out the following coordinates:\n$(5,10),\\: (10,20),\\: (15,30)$\n\nNow, let's put these coordinates into a table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$p$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$30$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNotice that for every $t$-value, we multiply by $2$ to get the corresponding $p$-value.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$p$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}2}\\cdot5=10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}2}\\cdot10=20$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}2}\\cdot15=30$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nHence, the equation that relates $p$ and $t$ is\n\n$$\np={\\color{blue}2}t\n$$\n\nSo, for $t=23$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= 2 \\cdot 23 = 46\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $23$ minutes, the charge level will be $46\\%$."] [:db/cas 17592186073996 :question/problem "From top to bottom, what are the missing numbers in the ratio table below?\n\n| $2$ | ? |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $8$ | $36$ |\n| ? | $54$ |" "From top to bottom, what are the missing numbers in the ratio table below?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">?</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$36$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">?</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$54$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186074024 :question/problem "| $3$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| ? | $6$ |\n| $12$ | $8$ |\n\nWhat number is missing from the ratio table?" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">?</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWhat number is missing from the ratio table?"] [:db/cas 17592186074384 :question/problem "From top to bottom, what are the missing numbers in the ratio table below?\n\n| $4$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $12$ | $?$ |\n| $?$ | $35$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$" "From top to bottom, what are the missing numbers in the ratio table below?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$?$</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$?$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$35$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n$$\n\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074385 :question/problem "Samantha needs $15$ olives to make $3$ pizzas. So, she creates a ratio table to determine how many olives she needs for $5$ and $7$ pizzas.\n\n| Olives | Pizzas |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $15$ | $3$ |\n| ? | $5$ |\n| ? | $7$ |\n\nFrom top to bottom, what are the missing numbers in the ratio table?" "Samantha needs $15$ olives to make $3$ pizzas. So, she creates a ratio table to determine how many olives she needs for $5$ and $7$ pizzas.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Olives</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pizzas</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">?</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">?</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFrom top to bottom, what are the missing numbers in the ratio table?"] [:db/cas 17592186074398 :question/problem "Paul has a car that can travel $80$ miles with $3$ gallons of gas. He creates a ratio table to determine the ratio of miles to gallons for different quantities.\n\n| Miles | Gallons |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $80$ | $3$ |\n| $160$ | $6$ |\n| ? | $12$ |\n\nHow many miles can he travel with $12$ gallons of gas?" "Paul has a car that can travel $80$ miles with $3$ gallons of gas. He creates a ratio table to determine the ratio of miles to gallons for different quantities.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Miles</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Gallons</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$80$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$160$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">?</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nHow many miles can he travel with $12$ gallons of gas?"] [:db/cas 17592186074417 :question/problem "By finding the missing values in the ratio table below, determine which of the following graphs represents the given relationship.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ |  |\n| $3$ |  |" "By finding the missing values in the ratio table below, determine which of the following graphs represents the given relationship.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-fill=\"#C0C1C2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-fill=\"#C0C1C2\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186074418 :question/problem "By finding the missing values in the ratio table below, determine which of the following graphs represents the given relationship.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ |  |\n| $3$ |  |" "By finding the missing values in the ratio table below, determine which of the following graphs represents the given relationship.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-fill=\"#C0C1C2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-fill=\"#C0C1C2\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186074935 :tutorial/content "![](images/72/72228072b08d97fec98735c251439585a5d897e635794ebb29e0974cb86d9f1f.png)\n\nThe table below shows the proof of the interior angles of a triangle theorem in a two-column format.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\overline{AB} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{DE}$ | Geometric construction |\n| 2 | $\\angle{ACD} \\cong \\angle{A}$ | Alternate interior angles theorem |\n| 3 | $m\\angle{ACD} = m\\angle{A}$ | Definition of congruent angles |\n| 4 | $\\angle{BCE} \\cong \\angle{B}$ | Alternate interior angles theorem |\n| 5 | $m\\angle{BCE} = m\\angle{B}$ | Definition of congruent angles |\n| 6 | $m\\angle{ACD} + m\\angle{ACB} + m\\angle{BCE} = m\\angle{DCE}$ | Angle addition postulate |\n| 7 | $m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ACB} + m\\angle{B} = m\\angle{DCE}$ | Substitution |\n| 8 | $m\\angle{DCE}=180^\\circ$ | Definition of a straight angle |\n| 9 | $m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ACB} + m\\angle{B} = 180^\\circ$ | Transitive property of equality |\n\nLet's now consider an alternative proof of this theorem using the corresponding angles postulate and the vertical angles theorem." "![](images/72/72228072b08d97fec98735c251439585a5d897e635794ebb29e0974cb86d9f1f.png)\n\nThe table below shows the proof of the interior angles of a triangle theorem in a two-column format.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AB} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{DE}$</td>\n<td>Geometric construction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\angle{ACD} \\cong \\angle{A}$</td>\n<td>Alternate interior angles theorem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{ACD} = m\\angle{A}$</td>\n<td>Definition of congruent angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$\\angle{BCE} \\cong \\angle{B}$</td>\n<td>Alternate interior angles theorem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{BCE} = m\\angle{B}$</td>\n<td>Definition of congruent angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{ACD} + m\\angle{ACB} + m\\angle{BCE} = m\\angle{DCE}$</td>\n<td>Angle addition postulate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ACB} + m\\angle{B} = m\\angle{DCE}$</td>\n<td>Substitution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">8</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{DCE}=180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Definition of a straight angle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">9</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ACB} + m\\angle{B} = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Transitive property of equality</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's now consider an alternative proof of this theorem using the corresponding angles postulate and the vertical angles theorem."] [:db/cas 17592186074937 :tutorial/content "![](images/6d/6df87ea11d90f73a6d35f7a37d15f46e4f807cbdaaeda0714d7a92cfc6afde67.png)\n\nThe table below shows our proof of the exterior angle theorem in a two-column format.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{FG} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{HJ}$ | Given |\n| 2 | $\\angle{IHJ} \\cong \\angle{F}$ | Corresponding angles postulate |\n| 3 | $m\\angle{IHJ} = m\\angle{F}$ | Definition of congruent angles |\n| 4 | $\\angle{JHG} \\cong \\angle{G}$ | Alternate interior angles theorem |\n| 5 | $m\\angle{JHG} = m\\angle{G}$ | Definition of congruent angles |\n| 6 | $m\\angle{IHJ} + m\\angle{JHG} = m\\angle{F} + m\\angle{G}$ | Addition principle |\n| 7 | $m\\angle{IHG} = m\\angle{IHJ} + m\\angle{JHG}$ | Angle addition postulate |\n| 8 | $m\\angle{IHG} = m\\angle{F} + m\\angle{G}$ | Transitive property of equality |\n\nLet's now consider a proof of the same theorem that uses the interior angles of a triangle theorem." "![](images/6d/6df87ea11d90f73a6d35f7a37d15f46e4f807cbdaaeda0714d7a92cfc6afde67.png)\n\nThe table below shows our proof of the exterior angle theorem in a two-column format.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{FG} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{HJ}$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\angle{IHJ} \\cong \\angle{F}$</td>\n<td>Corresponding angles postulate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{IHJ} = m\\angle{F}$</td>\n<td>Definition of congruent angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$\\angle{JHG} \\cong \\angle{G}$</td>\n<td>Alternate interior angles theorem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{JHG} = m\\angle{G}$</td>\n<td>Definition of congruent angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{IHJ} + m\\angle{JHG} = m\\angle{F} + m\\angle{G}$</td>\n<td>Addition principle</td>\n</tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{IHG} = m\\angle{IHJ} + m\\angle{JHG}$</td>\n<td>Angle addition postulate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">8</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{IHG} = m\\angle{F} + m\\angle{G}$</td>\n<td>Transitive property of equality</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's now consider a proof of the same theorem that uses the interior angles of a triangle theorem."] [:db/cas 17592186074938 :tutorial/content "![](images/51/510becfb1fe21822369dd65fb45168e9b9d08b076d9a51b712415b8243c61f7b.png)\n\nThe table below shows the proof of the isosceles triangle theorem in a two-column format.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\overline{AM}$ is a median of $\\triangle ABC$ | Geometric construction |\n| 2 | $\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$ | Given |\n| 3 | $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{BC}$ | Definition of median |\n| 4 | $BM = CM$ | Definition of a midpoint |\n| 5 | $\\overline{BM} \\cong \\overline{CM}$ | Definition of congruent segments |\n| 6 | $\\overline{AM} \\cong \\overline{AM}$ | Reflexive property of congruence |\n| 7 | $\\triangle ABM \\cong \\triangle ACM$ | SSS criterion |\n| 8 | $\\angle {B} \\cong \\angle {C}$ | CPCTC |" "![](images/51/510becfb1fe21822369dd65fb45168e9b9d08b076d9a51b712415b8243c61f7b.png)\n\nThe table below shows the proof of the isosceles triangle theorem in a two-column format.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AM}$ is a median of $\\triangle ABC$</td>\n<td>Geometric construction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{BC}$</td>\n<td>Definition of median</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$BM = CM$</td>\n<td>Definition of a midpoint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$\\overline{BM} \\cong \\overline{CM}$</td>\n<td>Definition of congruent segments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AM} \\cong \\overline{AM}$</td>\n<td>Reflexive property of congruence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$\\triangle ABM \\cong \\triangle ACM$</td>\n<td>SSS criterion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">8</td>\n<td>$\\angle {B} \\cong \\angle {C}$</td>\n<td>CPCTC</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186074939 :tutorial/content "We occasionally skip referencing the symmetric property when proving mathematical statements involving equality and congruence. This helps to make our proofs a bit shorter.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following two equations:\n\n$$\nx = y^2, \\qquad x = 2\n$$\n\nWe can construct a formal proof that $y^2 = 2$ using a so-called two-column format as follows:\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $x=y^2$ | Given |\n| 2 | $y^2 = x$ | Symmetric property of equality |\n| 3 | $x=2$ | Given |\n| 4 | $y^2=2$ | Transitive property of equality |\n\nThe last line is true because, from rows 2 and 3 respectively, we have\n\n$$\ny^2 = {\\color{red}{x}} \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad {\\color{red}{x}}=2\n$$\n\nand so by the transitive property of equality,\n$y^2 = 2$.\n\nWe can construct a shorter proof by omitting the second line:\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $x=y^2$ | Given |\n| 3 | $x=2$ | Given |\n| 3 | $y^2=2$ | Transitive property of equality |\n\nIn this proof, the symmetric property is still applied \"under the hood,\" but we don't explicitly refer to it." "We occasionally skip referencing the symmetric property when proving mathematical statements involving equality and congruence. This helps to make our proofs a bit shorter.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following two equations:\n\n$$\nx = y^2, \\qquad x = 2\n$$\n\nWe can construct a formal proof that $y^2 = 2$ using a so-called two-column format as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$x=y^2$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$y^2 = x$</td>\n<td>Symmetric property of equality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$x=2$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$y^2=2$</td>\n<td>Transitive property of equality</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe last line is true because, from rows 2 and 3 respectively, we have\n\n$$\ny^2 = {\\color{red}{x}} \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad {\\color{red}{x}}=2\n$$\n\nand so by the transitive property of equality,\n$y^2 = 2$.\n\nWe can construct a shorter proof by omitting the second line:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$x=y^2$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$x=2$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$y^2=2$</td>\n<td>Transitive property of equality</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nIn this proof, the symmetric property is still applied \"under the hood,\" but we don't explicitly refer to it."] [:db/cas 17592186074947 :tutorial/content "Sometimes we are given a graph and asked to find a value that is not shown directly on the graph. To solve this problem, we must first determine the equation of the graph.\n\nTo demonstrate, suppose a parking garage charges a flat entry fee plus a cost per hour.\nThe total cost $c$ (in dollars) after $h$ hours is shown in the graph below.\n\n![](images/56/56c832967f1a2cf782dcb0d47b1e968c074b30ea1fb9ce2c8dffc0cefadb161a.png)\n\nSuppose we want to find the total cost after $4.5$ hours.\n\nSince $h=4.5$ is not one of the labeled points on the graph, we cannot read the value directly.\nInstead, we will find the equation that relates $c$ and $h$.\n\nFrom the graph, we can pick out the following coordinates:\n$(0,6),\\: (4,10),\\: (6,12)$\n\nNow, let's put these coordinates into a table:\n\n| $h$ | $c$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0$ | $6$ |\n| $4$ | $10$ |\n| $6$ | $12$ |\n\nNotice that each $c$-value is $6$ more than the corresponding $h$-value.\n\n| $h$ | $c$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0 + {\\color{blue}6} = 6$ |\n| $4$ | $4 + {\\color{blue}6} = 10$ |\n| $6$ | $6 + {\\color{blue}6} = 12$ |\n\nHence, the equation that relates $c$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nc = h + {\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nSo, for $h=4.5$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc &= 4.5 + 6 = 10.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $4.5$ hours, the total cost is $\\$10.50$." "Sometimes we are given a graph and asked to find a value that is not shown directly on the graph. To solve this problem, we must first determine the equation of the graph.\n\nTo demonstrate, suppose a parking garage charges a flat entry fee plus a cost per hour.\nThe total cost $c$ (in dollars) after $h$ hours is shown in the graph below.\n\n![](images/56/56c832967f1a2cf782dcb0d47b1e968c074b30ea1fb9ce2c8dffc0cefadb161a.png)\n\nSuppose we want to find the total cost after $4.5$ hours.\n\nSince $h=4.5$ is not one of the labeled points on the graph, we cannot read the value directly.\nInstead, we will find the equation that relates $c$ and $h$.\n\nFrom the graph, we can pick out the following coordinates:\n$(0,6),\\: (4,10),\\: (6,12)$\n\nNow, let's put these coordinates into a table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$h$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$c$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNotice that each $c$-value is $6$ more than the corresponding $h$-value.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$h$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$c$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0 + {\\color{blue}6} = 6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4 + {\\color{blue}6} = 10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6 + {\\color{blue}6} = 12$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nHence, the equation that relates $c$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nc = h + {\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nSo, for $h=4.5$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc &= 4.5 + 6 = 10.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $4.5$ hours, the total cost is $\\$10.50$."] [:db/cas 17592186074956 :tutorial/content "We can use box models to divide numbers by two-digit numbers.\n\nLet's see how to use a box model by finding the value of $300 \\div 25$.\n\nWe start by writing our division in the following way. We place the dividend in a box and the divisor to the left of the box.\n\n|  |  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $25$ | $3$$0$$0$ |  |  |\n\nWe want to find how many times $25$ goes into $300$. But instead of trying to figure this out in a single step, let's break this problem up into a series of simpler steps.\n\nIn our first step, we will pick a multiple of $25$ that is easy to compute and is fairly large but no larger than $300$. We will choose the multiple $25 \\times {\\color{blue}10}$ because we can compute\n\n$$\n25 \\times {\\color{blue}10} = 250\n$$\n\nquickly and the result is no larger than $300$.\n\nTo organize our work so far, we write $\\color{blue}10$ above the box and subtract $25 \\times {\\color{blue}10}$ from $300$ inside the box.\n\n|  | $\\color{blue}10$ |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $25$ | $3$$0$$0$$-\\!$$2$$5$$0$$\\color{red}5$$\\color{red}0$ |  |  |\n\nThe result of the subtraction is ${\\color{red}50}$. This indicates that if we take $25$ a total of ${\\color{blue}10}$ times, then it will fit into $300$ with $\\color{red}50$ left over.\n\nNow, we repeat the process with a dividend of $\\color{red}50$ instead of $300$. To start the next round, we write $\\color{red}50$ in a new box to the right.\n\n|  | $10$ |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $25$ | $3$$0$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$5$$0$$\\color{red}5$$\\color{red}0$ | $\\color{red}5$$\\color{red}0$ |  |\n\nAgain, we are looking for a multiple of $25$ that is easy to compute and is no larger than ${\\color{red}50}$. We will choose the multiple $25 \\times {\\color{blue}2}$ because we can compute\n\n$$\n25 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 50\n$$\n\nquickly and the result is no larger than ${\\color{red}50}$.\n\nAs before, we write $\\color{blue}2$ above the box and subtract $25 \\times {\\color{blue}2}$ from ${\\color{red}50}$ inside the box.\n\n|  | $10$ | $\\color{blue}2$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $25$ | $3$$0$$0$$-\\!$$2$$5$$0$$\\color{red}5$$\\color{red}0$ | $\\color{red}5$$\\color{red}0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$0$$\\fbox{0}$ |  |\n\nWe get $\\fbox{0}$, so the division is done.\n\nTo find the quotient $300 \\div 25$, all we need to do is add the numbers on top of the boxes:\n\n$$\n10+2=12\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n300 \\div 25 = 12\\,\n$$" "We can use box models to divide numbers by two-digit numbers.\n\nLet's see how to use a box model by finding the value of $300 \\div 25$.\n\nWe start by writing our division in the following way. We place the dividend in a box and the divisor to the left of the box.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><ta