ter\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{blue}2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-\\!$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\fbox{0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe get $\\fbox{0}$, so the division is done.\n\nTo find the quotient $300 \\div 25$, all we need to do is add the numbers on top of the boxes:\n\n$$\n10+2=12\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n300 \\div 25 = 12\\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074968 :tutorial/content "Remember that we can place equivalent ratios in a ratio table, like the one below.\n\n| $\\color{blue}x$ | $\\color{red}y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\color{blue}1$ | $\\color{red}2$ |\n| $\\color{blue}2$ | $\\color{red}4$ |\n| $\\color{blue}4$ | $\\color{red}8$ |\n\nBy setting the values in the left column as $\\color{blue}x$-coordinates and the values in the right column as the corresponding $\\color{red}y$-coordinates, we can graph the points to represent a relationship between the ratios.\n\nIn this case, we have the following ordered pairs: $({\\color{blue}1},{\\color{red}2}),$ $({\\color{blue}2},{\\color{red}4}),$ $({\\color{blue}4},{\\color{red}8}).$\n\nPlotting these points on a coordinate plane, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/0c/0c4abb8d462730ed9b532feeca1e0a800f5376a978bfc799afb9d9a927ff310d.png)\n\nConnecting the points gives us a straight line! Plotting equivalent ratios will *always* give a straight line. Furthermore, the straight line will *always* pass through the origin $(0,0).$" "Remember that we can place equivalent ratios in a ratio table, like the one below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\color{blue}x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\color{red}y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nBy setting the values in the left column as $\\color{blue}x$-coordinates and the values in the right column as the corresponding $\\color{red}y$-coordinates, we can graph the points to represent a relationship between the ratios.\n\nIn this case, we have the following ordered pairs: $({\\color{blue}1},{\\color{red}2}),$ $({\\color{blue}2},{\\color{red}4}),$ $({\\color{blue}4},{\\color{red}8}).$\n\nPlotting these points on a coordinate plane, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/0c/0c4abb8d462730ed9b532feeca1e0a800f5376a978bfc799afb9d9a927ff310d.png)\n\nConnecting the points gives us a straight line! Plotting equivalent ratios will *always* give a straight line. Furthermore, the straight line will *always* pass through the origin $(0,0).$"] [:db/cas 17592186074975 :tutorial/content "We can represent two-variable equations using graphs. For example, consider the following equation:\n\n$$\ny = x + 5\n$$\n\nA point $(x, y)$ is a solution to this equation if substituting the $x$-value into the equation gives the correct $y$-value.\n\nTo find some solution points, we can choose several $x$-values and compute the corresponding $y$-values.\nNotice that:\n\n- If $x=0$, then $y=0 + 5 = 5$.\n- If $x=1$, then $y=1 + 5 = 6$.\n- If $x=2$, then $y=2 + 5 = 7$.\n- If $x=3$, then $y=3 + 5 = 8$.\n\nWe can organize these results in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0$ | $5$ |\n| $1$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $7$ |\n| $3$ | $8$ |\n\nSo, we have the following points:\n$(0,5),\\: (1,6),\\: (2,7),\\: (3,8)$\n\nTo graph the equation, we plot these points on the coordinate plane and draw a straight line through them.\n\n![](images/c6/c67b80c4aa1ed18929337792c7d9cdeea89f8b937f2e1fa29be767fcf627eaff.png)\n\nEvery point $(x,y)$ on the line satisfies the equation." "We can represent two-variable equations using graphs. For example, consider the following equation:\n\n$$\ny = x + 5\n$$\n\nA point $(x, y)$ is a solution to this equation if substituting the $x$-value into the equation gives the correct $y$-value.\n\nTo find some solution points, we can choose several $x$-values and compute the corresponding $y$-values.\nNotice that:\n\n- If $x=0$, then $y=0 + 5 = 5$.\n- If $x=1$, then $y=1 + 5 = 6$.\n- If $x=2$, then $y=2 + 5 = 7$.\n- If $x=3$, then $y=3 + 5 = 8$.\n\nWe can organize these results in the table below:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, we have the following points:\n$(0,5),\\: (1,6),\\: (2,7),\\: (3,8)$\n\nTo graph the equation, we plot these points on the coordinate plane and draw a straight line through them.\n\n![](images/c6/c67b80c4aa1ed18929337792c7d9cdeea89f8b937f2e1fa29be767fcf627eaff.png)\n\nEvery point $(x,y)$ on the line satisfies the equation."] [:db/cas 17592186075038 :tutorial/content "Let's take a look at the following equivalent ratios:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}10}, \\qquad {\\color{blue}10}:{\\color{#C0C1C2}\\boxed{ {\\phantom{00}}}}, \\qquad {\\color{blue}20}:{\\color{red}40}\n$$\n\nWe can place the equivalent ratios in a **ratio table**, such as the one below. In a ratio table, each row represents a ratio.\n\n| ${\\color{blue}5}$ | ${\\color{red}10}$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ${\\color{blue}10}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}\\textrm{?}}$ |\n| ${\\color{blue}20}$ | ${\\color{red}40}$ |\n\nOne number is missing. Let's figure out what it is.\n\nWe start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}10},$ which is the first ratio in the table. Writing this ratio as a fraction, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}5}}{{\\color{red}10}}.\n$$\n\nThe numerator represents the left-hand entry (${\\color{blue}5}$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry (${\\color{red}10}$).\n\nTo find the missing number, we need to make a numerator of ${\\color{blue}10}.$ We can do this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\times 2}{10\\times 2} & =\\frac{10}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now add this to our ratio table:\n\n| ${\\color{blue}5}$ | ${\\color{red}10}$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ${\\color{blue}10}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}20}$ |\n| ${\\color{blue}20}$ | ${\\color{red}40}$ |\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\color{red}20.$" "Let's take a look at the following equivalent ratios:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}10}, \\qquad {\\color{blue}10}:{\\color{#C0C1C2}\\boxed{ {\\phantom{00}}}}, \\qquad {\\color{blue}20}:{\\color{red}40}\n$$\n\nWe can place the equivalent ratios in a **ratio table**, such as the one below. In a ratio table, each row represents a ratio.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}5}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}10}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}\\textrm{?}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}20}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}40}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nOne number is missing. Let's figure out what it is.\n\nWe start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}10},$ which is the first ratio in the table. Writing this ratio as a fraction, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}5}}{{\\color{red}10}}.\n$$\n\nThe numerator represents the left-hand entry (${\\color{blue}5}$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry (${\\color{red}10}$).\n\nTo find the missing number, we need to make a numerator of ${\\color{blue}10}.$ We can do this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\times 2}{10\\times 2} & =\\frac{10}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now add this to our ratio table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}5}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}10}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}20}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}20}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}40}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\color{red}20.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078208 :question/problem "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $1.1$ | $1.3$ | $1.7$ | $2.5$ |\n\nApproximate $f'(x)$ at the point $x=0.2$ using a central difference approximation." "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nApproximate $f'(x)$ at the point $x=0.2$ using a central difference approximation."] [:db/cas 17592186078271 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If $z'(x)=0$ or $z'(x)$ is undefined, then we have a critical point.\n\n- If $z'(x)>0,$ then $z(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If $z'(x)<         0,$ then $z(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nThe function $z(x)$ has a relative extremum at $x=a$, if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- $z'(x)=0$ or it's undefined, and\n\n- $z'(x)$ changes its sign (going from left to right) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,5)$ | $5$ | $(5,8)$ | $8$ | $(8,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $p$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{not extr.}$ | $\\searrow$ |\n\nIn our case, we obtain relative extrema at $x=3,$ $x=4,$ and $x=5.$  \n\nNotice that $x=8$ is *not* a relative extremum because the sign of $z'(x)$ does not change either side of this critical point." "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If $z'(x)=0$ or $z'(x)$ is undefined, then we have a critical point.\n\n- If $z'(x)>0,$ then $z(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If $z'(x)<         0,$ then $z(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nThe function $z(x)$ has a relative extremum at $x=a$, if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- $z'(x)=0$ or it's undefined, and\n\n- $z'(x)$ changes its sign (going from left to right) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-\\infty,3)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(3,4)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(4,5)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(5,8)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(8,\\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$z'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{not def.}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\nearrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{max}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\searrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{min}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\nearrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{max}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\searrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{not extr.}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\searrow$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nIn our case, we obtain relative extrema at $x=3,$ $x=4,$ and $x=5.$  \n\nNotice that $x=8$ is *not* a relative extremum because the sign of $z'(x)$ does not change either side of this critical point."] [:db/cas 17592186078366 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' =7x-2y, \\qquad y(1)=-4\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $\\Delta y$ in the table below if the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1}{10}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  |  | $\\, ? \\,$ |" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' =7x-2y, \\qquad y(1)=-4\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $\\Delta y$ in the table below if the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1}{10}?$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, \\Delta y \\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, ? \\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186078366 :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=-4,$ we fill $y=-4$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-4$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =7x-2y \\\\  & =7\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-4$ | $15$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =15\\cdot \\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-4$ | $15$ | $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ |" "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=-4,$ we fill $y=-4$ into the table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, \\Delta y \\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =7x-2y \\\\  & =7\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, \\Delta y \\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =15\\cdot \\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, \\Delta y \\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{3}{2}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186078377 :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2-1 = 1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {3}^{2}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =18-1 \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =17\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ | $17$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =3+17 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ | $17$ |\n| $2$ | $20$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2) \\approx 20.$" "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2-1 = 1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, \\Delta y \\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {3}^{2}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =18-1 \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, \\Delta y \\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =17\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, \\Delta y \\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =3+17 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, \\Delta y \\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2) \\approx 20.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078381 :question/problem "Which missing $xy$-pair would make the following rule a function?\n\n| **$x$** | $-3$ | $-1$ | ? | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $4$ | ? | $0$ | $2$ | $1$ | $7$ |\n\nI. $(1,2)$\n\nII. $(7,3)$\n\nIII. $(-1,5)$" "Which missing $xy$-pair would make the following rule a function?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">?</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">?</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nI. $(1,2)$\n\nII. $(7,3)$\n\nIII. $(-1,5)$"] [:db/cas 17592186078381 :question/worked-solution "A rule is a function if and only if every input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\n- If we substitute the $xy$-pair $(1,2)$ into our table, we get a function.\n\n| **$x$** | $-3$ | $-1$ | $\\color{blue}1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $4$ | $2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $1$ | $7$ |\n\nNotice that all values in the first row are *distinct* (i.e., different). Every input value is assigned to *precisely one* output value.\n\n- None of the other $xy$-pairs gives rules that are functions. So, for example, substituting the $xy$-pair $(-1,5)$ gives a rule that is *not* a function. \n\n| **$x$** | $-3$ | $\\color{red}-1$ | $\\color{red}-1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $4$ | $5$ | $0$ | $2$ | $1$ | $7$ |\n\nNotice that the first row contains the value $\\color{red}x=-1$ twice. The input value $-1$ is assigned to *more than one* output value ($4$ and $5$)." "A rule is a function if and only if every input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\n- If we substitute the $xy$-pair $(1,2)$ into our table, we get a function.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNotice that all values in the first row are *distinct* (i.e., different). Every input value is assigned to *precisely one* output value.\n\n- None of the other $xy$-pairs gives rules that are functions. So, for example, substituting the $xy$-pair $(-1,5)$ gives a rule that is *not* a function. \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNotice that the first row contains the value $\\color{red}x=-1$ twice. The input value $-1$ is assigned to *more than one* output value ($4$ and $5$)."] [:db/cas 17592186078864 :question/problem "A function $g(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| **$x$** | $-1.05$ | $-1$ | $-0.95$ | $-0.9$ | $-0.85$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $3.4$ | $2.5$ | $1.6$ | $2.1$ | $2.7$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nApproximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=-1$ using a central difference approximation." "A function $g(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1.05$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.95$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.85$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nApproximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=-1$ using a central difference approximation."] [:db/cas 17592186078878 :question/problem "Consider a rule between $x$ and $y$ given by the table below.\n\n| **$x$** | $12$ | $9$ | $7$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $15$ | $12$ | $9$ | $6$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The rule is a function\n\nII. The rule is **not** a function\n\nIII. The rule maps the input $x=15$ to the output $y=12$" "Consider a rule between $x$ and $y$ given by the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The rule is a function\n\nII. The rule is **not** a function\n\nIII. The rule maps the input $x=15$ to the output $y=12$"] [:db/cas 17592186078955 :question/problem "Which missing $xy$-pair would make the following rule a function?\n\n| **$x$** | $-5$ | ? | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $25$ | ? | $1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |" "Which missing $xy$-pair would make the following rule a function?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">?</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">?</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186079687 :tutorial/content "Let's return to the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x+y+1, \\qquad y(0)=2\n$$\n\nWe will now use Euler's method to approximate the value of $y(1).$ \n\nOur problem can be viewed schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d4/d4d3cc042ae1c6dc451c8b2136343056f7c2dc7d420da0cb4d331da37371819d.png)\n\nSince we know $y({\\color{red}{0}})$ and wish to estimate $y({\\color{blue}{1}}),$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\n\\Delta x = {\\color{blue}{1}}-{\\color{red}{0}} = 1.\n$$\n\nIt's best to use a table like the one below when applying Euler's method. First, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNow, using the differential equation, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =x+y+1 \\\\  & =0+2+1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add $y'= 3$ to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $3$ |  |\n\nNext, using Euler's method, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{'}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add $\\Delta y = 3$ to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n\nFinally, to get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =2+3 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these entries to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $1$ | $5$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx 5.$" "Let's return to the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x+y+1, \\qquad y(0)=2\n$$\n\nWe will now use Euler's method to approximate the value of $y(1).$ \n\nOur problem can be viewed schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d4/d4d3cc042ae1c6dc451c8b2136343056f7c2dc7d420da0cb4d331da37371819d.png)\n\nSince we know $y({\\color{red}{0}})$ and wish to estimate $y({\\color{blue}{1}}),$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\n\\Delta x = {\\color{blue}{1}}-{\\color{red}{0}} = 1.\n$$\n\nIt's best to use a table like the one below when applying Euler's method. First, we add the initial condition data:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, \\Delta y \\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, using the differential equation, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =x+y+1 \\\\  & =0+2+1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add $y'= 3$ to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, \\Delta y \\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNext, using Euler's method, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{'}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add $\\Delta y = 3$ to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, \\Delta y \\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, to get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =2+3 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these entries to our table gives the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, \\Delta y \\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx 5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186079688 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x+y+1, \\qquad y(0)=2\n$$\n\nCarrying out Euler's method, we got the following table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $1$ | $5$ |  |  |\n\nWe can view our approximation in the coordinate plane as follows:\n\n![](images/24/24de021a22af84fb22c0c4fef9894be15433a2111306e073c79fff8081db9815.png)\n\nThe beauty of Euler's method is that we can apply it iteratively, meaning that we can continue to make approximations to the solution curve in a systematic way. \n\nFor example, it can be shown that by applying one more iteration of Euler's method, we get the following:\n\n![](images/66/662e414e621a6830abe1b75e41a0778c55b632f1f57339a4b0463cd362021bf0.png)\n\nWe will learn how to apply multiple iterations of Euler's method in a future lesson." "Let's go back to the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x+y+1, \\qquad y(0)=2\n$$\n\nCarrying out Euler's method, we got the following table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, \\Delta y \\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe can view our approximation in the coordinate plane as follows:\n\n![](images/24/24de021a22af84fb22c0c4fef9894be15433a2111306e073c79fff8081db9815.png)\n\nThe beauty of Euler's method is that we can apply it iteratively, meaning that we can continue to make approximations to the solution curve in a systematic way. \n\nFor example, it can be shown that by applying one more iteration of Euler's method, we get the following:\n\n![](images/66/662e414e621a6830abe1b75e41a0778c55b632f1f57339a4b0463cd362021bf0.png)\n\nWe will learn how to apply multiple iterations of Euler's method in a future lesson."] [:db/cas 17592186079695 :tutorial/content "When a rule is represented as a table, we have a function if and only if all values in the *first row* are *distinct* (i.e., different).\n\nLet's consider some examples.\n\nThe rule defined by the following table is a function. Notice that all values in the first row are distinct. Every input value is assigned to exactly one output value.\n\n| **$x$** | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $0$ | $7$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, the rule defined by the following table is *not* a function. Notice that the first row contains the value $\\color{red}3$ twice. The input value $3$ is assigned to two output values ($0$ and $-1$).\n\n| **$x$** | $1$ | $2$ | $\\color{red}3$ | $4$ | $\\color{red}3$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $0$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $5$ |" "When a rule is represented as a table, we have a function if and only if all values in the *first row* are *distinct* (i.e., different).\n\nLet's consider some examples.\n\nThe rule defined by the following table is a function. Notice that all values in the first row are distinct. Every input value is assigned to exactly one output value.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, the rule defined by the following table is *not* a function. Notice that the first row contains the value $\\color{red}3$ twice. The input value $3$ is assigned to two output values ($0$ and $-1$).\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186079725 :tutorial/content "Each type of polynomial of degree between $0$ and $5$ has a specific name:\n\n| Degree | Name | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | Constant | $7$ |\n| $1$ | Linear | $3x + 2$ |\n| $2$ | Quadratic | $2x^2 - 6x + 1$ |\n| $3$ | Cubic | $x^3 + 4x^2 + 9x$ |\n| $4$ | Quartic | $3x^4 - 5x^2$ |\n| $5$ | Quintic | $7x^5 + 3x^4 - x^4 + x - 6$ |" "Each type of polynomial of degree between $0$ and $5$ has a specific name:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Degree</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Example</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td>Constant</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td>Linear</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3x + 2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td>Quadratic</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2x^2 - 6x + 1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td>Cubic</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x^3 + 4x^2 + 9x$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td>Quartic</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3x^4 - 5x^2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td>Quintic</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7x^5 + 3x^4 - x^4 + x - 6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186082895 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u \\to \\infty$ when $x \\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n=\n\\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{u^2} \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{a}-\\frac{1}{1}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we substitute $u=x+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u \\to \\infty$ when $x \\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x+1$ is as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$u$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n=\n\\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{u^2} \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{a}-\\frac{1}{1}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186082905 :question/problem "| **$x$** | $-8$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $7$ | $3$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-3$ | $-4$ | $-6$ |\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ defined by the table above, what is the value of $f^{-1}(3)?$" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ defined by the table above, what is the value of $f^{-1}(3)?$"] [:db/cas 17592186082940 :question/problem "What is the range of the function $f$ defined by the following table?\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $3$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $2$ | $0$ |" "What is the range of the function $f$ defined by the following table?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186082984 :question/problem "The table below defines the function $g(x).$ Given that $g(x)$ is an even function, find the value of $a+b.$\n\n| **$x$** | $-5$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $20$ | $11$ | $-4$ | $a-3$ | $11$ | $b+5$ |" "The table below defines the function $g(x).$ Given that $g(x)$ is an even function, find the value of $a+b.$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$a-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$b+5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186083002 :question/worked-solution "Notice that, this time, it's the *lower* limit of integration that's unbounded.  \n\nFirst, we substitute $u=x-4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty$ when $x\\to -\\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x-4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-4$ | $-\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}    \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n=\\int_{-\\infty}^{-4} \\dfrac{1}{u^2}  \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-4)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that, this time, it's the *lower* limit of integration that's unbounded.  \n\nFirst, we substitute $u=x-4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty$ when $x\\to -\\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x-4$ is as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\infty$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$u$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\infty$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}    \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n=\\int_{-\\infty}^{-4} \\dfrac{1}{u^2}  \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-4)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186083006 :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u = \\cos x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -\\sin x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\textrm d u = \\sin x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{0}\\frac{-\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1}^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u) = \\dfrac{1}{u} \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad\\textrm{ as }\\quad u\\rightarrow 0^+.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to0^+.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u||}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[0-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|] \\\\  & =-(-\\infty ) \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the integral is divergent." "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u = \\cos x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -\\sin x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\textrm d u = \\sin x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$u$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{0}\\frac{-\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1}^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u) = \\dfrac{1}{u} \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad\\textrm{ as }\\quad u\\rightarrow 0^+.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to0^+.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u||}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[0-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|] \\\\  & =-(-\\infty ) \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the integral is divergent."] [:db/cas 17592186083066 :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ represent the profit on a draw in this game. On each draw in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- drawing $1,2,3,4$, the player loses $\\$1,$ so we have $p=-1.$\n\n- drawing $5$, the player wins $\\$5,$ so we have $p=5.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\nP(-1)=\\dfrac{4}{5}, \\qquad P(5)=\\dfrac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single draw, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $p$ | $-1$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(p)$ | $\\dfrac{4}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\textrm E[p] &= -1 \\cdot f(-1) + 5 \\cdot f(5)  \\\\[5pt]\n&= -1 \\left( \\dfrac{4}{5} \\right) + 5 \\left( \\dfrac{1}{5} \\right)  \\\\[5pt]\n\t&=-\\dfrac{4}{5} + 1 \\\\[3pt]\n\t&=\\dfrac{1}{5} \\\\[3pt]\n\t&=0.2\n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit at each draw in this game is $\\$0.20$." "Let $p$ represent the profit on a draw in this game. On each draw in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- drawing $1,2,3,4$, the player loses $\\$1,$ so we have $p=-1.$\n\n- drawing $5$, the player wins $\\$5,$ so we have $p=5.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\nP(-1)=\\dfrac{4}{5}, \\qquad P(5)=\\dfrac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single draw, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(p)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{4}{5}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{5}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\textrm E[p] &= -1 \\cdot f(-1) + 5 \\cdot f(5)  \\\\[5pt]\n&= -1 \\left( \\dfrac{4}{5} \\right) + 5 \\left( \\dfrac{1}{5} \\right)  \\\\[5pt]\n\t&=-\\dfrac{4}{5} + 1 \\\\[3pt]\n\t&=\\dfrac{1}{5} \\\\[3pt]\n\t&=0.2\n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit at each draw in this game is $\\$0.20$."] [:db/cas 17592186083108 :question/problem "The rolls of an unfair tetrahedral die follow the probability mass function $f(x)$ as shown in the table below. What is the expected value of one roll of the die?\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ |" "The rolls of an unfair tetrahedral die follow the probability mass function $f(x)$ as shown in the table below. What is the expected value of one roll of the die?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{3}{10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{2}{5}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{5}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186083109 :question/problem "Several children were asked about how many pets they have. The results were recorded below. If $X$ is the number of pets that a randomly selected child has, what is the expected value of $X?$\n\n| Number of pets | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Number of Children | $5$ | $6$ | $5$ |" "Several children were asked about how many pets they have. The results were recorded below. If $X$ is the number of pets that a randomly selected child has, what is the expected value of $X?$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Number of pets</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Number of Children</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186083109 :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x \\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the number of pets, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of children is $5+6+5=16,$ so the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0)=f(0) & =\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ P(X=1)=f(1) & =\\frac{6}{16}=\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ P(X=2)=f(2) & =\\frac{5}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\dfrac{5}{16}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{16}$ |\n\nNow, summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2) \\\\  & =0\\left(\\frac{5}{16}\\right)+1\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)+2\\left(\\frac{5}{16}\\right) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{3}{8}+\\frac{10}{16} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{6}{16}+\\frac{10}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+6+10}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{16} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x \\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the number of pets, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of children is $5+6+5=16,$ so the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0)=f(0) & =\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ P(X=1)=f(1) & =\\frac{6}{16}=\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ P(X=2)=f(2) & =\\frac{5}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{5}{16}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{3}{8}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{5}{16}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2) \\\\  & =0\\left(\\frac{5}{16}\\right)+1\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)+2\\left(\\frac{5}{16}\\right) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{3}{8}+\\frac{10}{16} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{6}{16}+\\frac{10}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+6+10}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{16} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186083110 :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of the constant term}}{\\textrm{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $4$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of }{4}}{\\textrm{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm1, \\pm 2, \\pm 4\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(0,4),$ are $1$ and $2.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $\\left(x-1\\right)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-4$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $\\left(x-1\\right)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\left( x - 1\\right) (x^2 - 4).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-1)({x}^{2}-4) \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+2)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-2, 1, 2.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-2+1+2 =1.\n$$" "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of the constant term}}{\\textrm{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $4$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of }{4}}{\\textrm{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm1, \\pm 2, \\pm 4\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(0,4),$ are $1$ and $2.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $\\left(x-1\\right)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$\\color{gray}\\downarrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$-4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $\\left(x-1\\right)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\left( x - 1\\right) (x^2 - 4).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-1)({x}^{2}-4) \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+2)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-2, 1, 2.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-2+1+2 =1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186083126 :question/problem "The birth weights of newborns in a given population are normally distributed with a mean of $3\\,543$ grams and a standard deviation of $609$ grams. What is the probability that a randomly selected newborn baby weighs more than $3\\,100$ grams but less than $3\\,600$? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.01 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.02 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.03 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -0.8 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.2090 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.2061 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.2033 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -0.7 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.2389 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.2358 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.2327 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -0.6 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.2709 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.2676 \\,$ </td><td al