lign=\"center\">$\\,0.15\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.20\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.25\\,$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">${Φ}^{-1}(p)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,-2.3263\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,-1.8808\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,-1.6449\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,-1.2816\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,-1.0364\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,-0.8416\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,-0.6745\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.4\\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.6736\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.6772\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.6808\\,$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.5\\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.7088\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.7123\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.7157\\,$</td></tr>\n<tr> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.6\\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.7422\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.7454\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.7486\\,$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186083977 :question/problem "The duration of feline pregnancies is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ days and a standard deviation of $5$ days. Additionally, $20\\%$ of the pregnancies have a duration lower than $59$ days. Find the mean $\\mu $, rounded to the nearest day.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |" "The duration of feline pregnancies is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ days and a standard deviation of $5$ days. Additionally, $20\\%$ of the pregnancies have a duration lower than $59$ days. Find the mean $\\mu $, rounded to the nearest day.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$p$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.01\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.03\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.05\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.10\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.15\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.20\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.25\\,$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">${Φ}^{-1}(p)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,-2.3263\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,-1.8808\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,-1.6449\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,-1.2816\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,-1.0364\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,-0.8416\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,-0.6745\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186084414 :tutorial/content "The values of the trigonometric ratios for $0^\\circ$ angles are the following:\n\n| Trigonometric ratio | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\sin \\left(0^\\circ\\right)$ | $0$ |\n| $\\cos \\left(0^\\circ\\right)$ | $1$ |\n| $\\tan \\left(0^\\circ\\right)$ | $0$ |\n\nWhy is this so? If we look at the right triangle below, we can imagine making the angle $\\theta$ smaller by moving the vertex $C$ towards $A.$ \n\n![](images/9b/9bee0468c989067e12648e538788bcd8c7fb879e112000a942a1ee6f41d95c7d.png)\n\nAs the angle $\\theta$ gets closer and closer to zero, the opposite side $AC$ also approaches zero. Also, the hypotenuse $BC$ and the adjacent side $AB$ become nearly the same length.\n\nSo, as $\\theta$ approaches zero (which we can write as $\\theta \\rightarrow 0^\\circ$) we have\n\n$$\nAC \\approx 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad BC \\approx AB.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, when $\\theta = 0^\\circ$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({0}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AC}{BC}=0, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({0}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AB}{BC}=1, \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({0}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AC}{AB}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The values of the trigonometric ratios for $0^\\circ$ angles are the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Trigonometric ratio</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\sin \\left(0^\\circ\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\cos \\left(0^\\circ\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\tan \\left(0^\\circ\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWhy is this so? If we look at the right triangle below, we can imagine making the angle $\\theta$ smaller by moving the vertex $C$ towards $A.$ \n\n![](images/9b/9bee0468c989067e12648e538788bcd8c7fb879e112000a942a1ee6f41d95c7d.png)\n\nAs the angle $\\theta$ gets closer and closer to zero, the opposite side $AC$ also approaches zero. Also, the hypotenuse $BC$ and the adjacent side $AB$ become nearly the same length.\n\nSo, as $\\theta$ approaches zero (which we can write as $\\theta \\rightarrow 0^\\circ$) we have\n\n$$\nAC \\approx 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad BC \\approx AB.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, when $\\theta = 0^\\circ$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({0}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AC}{BC}=0, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({0}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AB}{BC}=1, \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({0}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AC}{AB}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186084415 :tutorial/content "The values of the trigonometric ratios for $90^\\circ$ angles are the following:\n\n| Trigonometric ratio | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\sin \\left(90^\\circ\\right)$ | $1$ |\n| $\\cos \\left(90^\\circ\\right)$ | $0$ |\n| $\\tan \\left(90^\\circ\\right)$ | Undefined |\n\nTo understand where the values come from, this time, we can imagine increasing the value of the angle $\\theta$ by moving the vertex $B$ towards $A.$ \n\n![](images/53/53d538ce2eca6af579b556bdac72d9fba9ba5e817de38ff73236a312b401c4cf.png)\n\nAs $B$ moves towards $A,$ the adjacent side $AB$ gets very close to zero, and the angle $\\theta$ gets very close to $90^\\circ.$ Also, the hypotenuse $BC$ and the opposite side $AC$ become nearly the same length.\n\nSo, as $\\theta$ approaches $90^\\circ$ (which we can write as $\\theta \\rightarrow 90^\\circ$) we have\n\n$$\nAB \\approx 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad BC \\approx AC.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, when $\\theta = 90^\\circ,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({90}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AC}{BC}=1, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({90}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AB}{BC}=0, \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({90}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AC}{AB}=\\frac{AC}{0}=\\text{undefined.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when calculating the tangent, we get a $0$ in the denominator. We know that we can't divide by zero. Therefore, the tangent of a right angle is said to be undefined." "The values of the trigonometric ratios for $90^\\circ$ angles are the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Trigonometric ratio</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\sin \\left(90^\\circ\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\cos \\left(90^\\circ\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\tan \\left(90^\\circ\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Undefined</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo understand where the values come from, this time, we can imagine increasing the value of the angle $\\theta$ by moving the vertex $B$ towards $A.$ \n\n![](images/53/53d538ce2eca6af579b556bdac72d9fba9ba5e817de38ff73236a312b401c4cf.png)\n\nAs $B$ moves towards $A,$ the adjacent side $AB$ gets very close to zero, and the angle $\\theta$ gets very close to $90^\\circ.$ Also, the hypotenuse $BC$ and the opposite side $AC$ become nearly the same length.\n\nSo, as $\\theta$ approaches $90^\\circ$ (which we can write as $\\theta \\rightarrow 90^\\circ$) we have\n\n$$\nAB \\approx 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad BC \\approx AC.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, when $\\theta = 90^\\circ,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({90}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AC}{BC}=1, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({90}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AB}{BC}=0, \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({90}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AC}{AB}=\\frac{AC}{0}=\\text{undefined.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when calculating the tangent, we get a $0$ in the denominator. We know that we can't divide by zero. Therefore, the tangent of a right angle is said to be undefined."] [:db/cas 17592186084417 :tutorial/content "The table below summarizes the values of some *special* angles in degrees and radians.\n\n| Degrees | $\\:30^\\circ\\:$ | $\\:45^\\circ\\:$ | $\\:60^\\circ\\:$ | $\\:90^\\circ\\:$ | $\\:120^\\circ\\:$ | $\\:135^\\circ\\:$ | $\\:150^\\circ\\:$ | $\\:180^\\circ\\:$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Radians | $\\dfrac{\\pi}6$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}4$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}3$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}2$ | $\\dfrac{2\\pi}3$ | $\\dfrac{3\\pi}4$ | $\\dfrac{5\\pi}6$ | $\\pi$ |\n|  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| Degrees | $\\:210^\\circ\\:$ | $\\:225^\\circ\\:$ | $\\:240^\\circ\\:$ | $\\:270^\\circ\\:$ | $\\:300^\\circ\\:$ | $\\:315^\\circ\\:$ | $\\:330^\\circ\\:$ | $\\:360^\\circ\\:$ |\n| Radians | $\\dfrac{7\\pi}6$ | $\\dfrac{5\\pi}4$ | $\\dfrac{4\\pi}3$ | $\\dfrac{3\\pi}2$ | $\\dfrac{5\\pi}3$ | $\\dfrac{7\\pi}4$ | $\\dfrac{11\\pi}6$ | $2\\pi$ |\n\nRadians might seem a weird way of measuring angles now, but as you learn about trigonometry, you'll come to realize that they can be very useful and convenient." "The table below summarizes the values of some *special* angles in degrees and radians.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Degrees</th>\n<td>$\\:30^\\circ\\:$</td>\n<td>$\\:45^\\circ\\:$</td>\n<td>$\\:60^\\circ\\:$</td>\n<td data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$\\:90^\\circ\\:$</td>\n<td>$\\:120^\\circ\\:$</td>\n<td>$\\:135^\\circ\\:$</td>\n<td>$\\:150^\\circ\\:$</td>\n<td data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$\\:180^\\circ\\:$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Radians</th>\n<td>$\\dfrac{\\pi}6$</td> <td>$\\dfrac{\\pi}4$</td>\n<td>$\\dfrac{\\pi}3$</td>\n<td data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$\\dfrac{\\pi}2$</td>\n<td>$\\dfrac{2\\pi}3$</td>\n<td>$\\dfrac{3\\pi}4$</td>\n<td>$\\dfrac{5\\pi}6$</td>\n<td data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$\\pi$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td colspan=\"10\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Degrees</th>\n<td>$\\:210^\\circ\\:$</td>\n<td>$\\:225^\\circ\\:$</td>\n<td>$\\:240^\\circ\\:$</td>\n<td data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$\\:270^\\circ\\:$</td>\n<td>$\\:300^\\circ\\:$</td>\n<td>$\\:315^\\circ\\:$</td>\n<td>$\\:330^\\circ\\:$</td>\n<td data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$\\:360^\\circ\\:$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Radians</th>\n<td>$\\dfrac{7\\pi}6$</td> <td>$\\dfrac{5\\pi}4$</td>\n<td>$\\dfrac{4\\pi}3$</td>\n<td data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$\\dfrac{3\\pi}2$</td>\n<td>$\\dfrac{5\\pi}3$</td>\n<td>$\\dfrac{7\\pi}4$</td>\n<td>$\\dfrac{11\\pi}6$</td>\n<td data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$2\\pi$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nRadians might seem a weird way of measuring angles now, but as you learn about trigonometry, you'll come to realize that they can be very useful and convenient."] [:db/cas 17592186087424 :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)$ defined by the following table? \n\n| $x$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ | $\\,6\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |" "What is the domain of the function $f(x)$ defined by the following table? \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td>$\\,-2\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,-1\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,4\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,6\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186087518 :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=3t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -3\\leq t \\leq 3,\n$$\n\nfill in the following table of values.\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |" "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=3t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -3\\leq t \\leq 3,\n$$\n\nfill in the following table of values.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$t$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-f