">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, we work out the $y$-values using $y=t^3.$ This gives\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$t$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-27$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$27$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186087522 :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=t(t-6).$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t^3.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ |\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/f1/f15f25af0c4804dc811a0ab231ba2ef3ecce2ecd12501a1f2d8b9e34b71a505b.png)" "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=t(t-6).$ This gives\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$t$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t^3.$ This gives\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$t$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/f1/f15f25af0c4804dc811a0ab231ba2ef3ecce2ecd12501a1f2d8b9e34b71a505b.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186087581 :question/worked-solution "The remainder theorem states that when $p(x)$ is divided by\n\n$$\n(3x-2)= 3\\left(x-\\dfrac23\\right)\n$$\n\nthe remainder equals\n\n$$\np\\left(\\dfrac23\\right)\n$$\n\nEvaluating\n\n$$\np\\left(\\dfrac23\\right)\n$$\n\ndirectly gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} p\\left(\\dfrac23\\right) & =3\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac23\\right)^2 +7\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac23\\right) - 4 \\\\[5pt] & =3\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac49\\right) +\\dfrac{14}{3} - 4 \\\\[5pt] & =\\dfrac{12}{9}+\\dfrac{2}{3} \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac{12+ 3\\cdot 2}{9} \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac{18}{9} \\\\[5pt] & = 2. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, when $p(x)$ is divided by\n\n$$\n3\\left(x-\\dfrac23\\right)\n$$\n\nthe remainder is $2$.\n\n**Note:** We can check the answer by carrying out synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}\\frac23$ | $3$ | $7$ | $\\!\\!\\!\\!-4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $2$ | $6$ |\n|  | $3$ | $9$ | $2$ |" "The remainder theorem states that when $p(x)$ is divided by\n\n$$\n(3x-2)= 3\\left(x-\\dfrac23\\right)\n$$\n\nthe remainder equals\n\n$$\np\\left(\\dfrac23\\right)\n$$\n\nEvaluating\n\n$$\np\\left(\\dfrac23\\right)\n$$\n\ndirectly gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} p\\left(\\dfrac23\\right) & =3\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac23\\right)^2 +7\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac23\\right) - 4 \\\\[5pt] & =3\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac49\\right) +\\dfrac{14}{3} - 4 \\\\[5pt] & =\\dfrac{12}{9}+\\dfrac{2}{3} \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac{12+ 3\\cdot 2}{9} \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac{18}{9} \\\\[5pt] & = 2. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, when $p(x)$ is divided by\n\n$$\n3\\left(x-\\dfrac23\\right)\n$$\n\nthe remainder is $2$.\n\n**Note:** We can check the answer by carrying out synthetic division:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}\\frac23$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$\\!\\!\\!\\!-4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$\\color{gray}\\downarrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186087651 :question/worked-solution "Let $T$ be the time it takes for John and Mary to be $3\\,\\textrm{km}$ apart.\n\nWe fill in a speed-time-distance table:\n\n| Person | Speed (km/hr) | Time (hr) | Distance (km) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Mary** | $5$ | $T$ | $5T$ |\n| **John** | $7$ | $T$ | $7T$ |\n\nAfter $T$ hours, they will have traveled a combined distance of $3\\,\\textrm{km}$. Therefore, we can set the sum of the distances equal to $3$ and solve for time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5T + 7T &= 3 \\\\\n12T &= 3 \\\\\nT &= \\frac{3}{12} \\\\\nT &= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt will take\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nhours for John and Mary to be $3\\,\\textrm{km}$ apart. If we convert this time to minutes, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{hr} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{hr} \\times  {\\displaystyle \\frac{60 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{min}}{\\text{hr}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{\\text{hr}} \\times  {\\displaystyle \\frac{60 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{min}}{\\cancel{\\text{hr}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} \\times  60 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{min} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 15 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{min} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it will take $15$ minutes for them to be $3\\,\\textrm{km}$ apart." "Let $T$ be the time it takes for John and Mary to be $3\\,\\textrm{km}$ apart.\n\nWe fill in a speed-time-distance table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Person</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Speed (km/hr)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Time (hr)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Distance (km)</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>Mary</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5T$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>John</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7T$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nAfter $T$ hours, they will have traveled a combined distance of $3\\,\\textrm{km}$. Therefore, we can set the sum of the distances equal to $3$ and solve for time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5T + 7T &= 3 \\\\\n12T &= 3 \\\\\nT &= \\frac{3}{12} \\\\\nT &= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt will take\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nhours for John and Mary to be $3\\,\\textrm{km}$ apart. If we convert this time to minutes, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{hr} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{hr} \\times  {\\displaystyle \\frac{60 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{min}}{\\text{hr}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{\\text{hr}} \\times  {\\displaystyle \\frac{60 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{min}}{\\cancel{\\text{hr}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} \\times  60 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{min} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 15 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{min} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it will take $15$ minutes for them to be $3\\,\\textrm{km}$ apart."] [:db/cas 17592186087653 :question/worked-solution "Let's call $N$ the first train (heading toward New York) and $C$ the second train (heading toward Chicago).\n\nLet $T$ be the number of hours from when $N$ leaves Chicago to when it meets $C$. Then, $C$ will have been traveling for $T-3$ hours because it left the station three hours after the train $N$.\n\nWe fill in a speed-time-distance table:\n\n| Train | Speed (km/hr) | Time (hr) | Distance (km) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| N | $180$ | $T$ | $180T$ |\n| C | $150$ | $T-3$ | $150(T-3)$ |\n\nAfter $T$ hours, the trains have traveled a combined distance of $1\\,200$ kilometers. Therefore, we can set the sum of the distances equal to $1\\,200$ and solve for time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n180T + 150(T - 3) &= 1200 \\\\\n180T + 150T - 450 &= 1200 \\\\\n330T - 450 &= 1200 \\\\\n330T &= 1650 \\\\\nT &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the trains will pass each other $5$ hours after $N$ leaves Chicago. Since $N$ leaves Chicago at $1$ pm, trains will pass each other at $6$ pm." "Let's call $N$ the first train (heading toward New York) and $C$ the second train (heading toward Chicago).\n\nLet $T$ be the number of hours from when $N$ leaves Chicago to when it meets $C$. Then, $C$ will have been traveling for $T-3$ hours because it left the station three hours after the train $N$.\n\nWe fill in a speed-time-distance table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Train</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Speed (km/hr)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Time (hr)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Distance (km)</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">N</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$180$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$180T$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$150$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$T-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$150(T-3)$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nAfter $T$ hours, the trains have traveled a combined distance of $1\\,200$ kilometers. Therefore, we can set the sum of the distances equal to $1\\,200$ and solve for time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n180T + 150(T - 3) &= 1200 \\\\\n180T + 150T - 450 &= 1200 \\\\\n330T - 450 &= 1200 \\\\\n330T &= 1650 \\\\\nT &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the trains will pass each other $5$ hours after $N$ leaves Chicago. Since $N$ leaves Chicago at $1$ pm, trains will pass each other at $6$ pm."] [:db/cas 17592186088220 :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f$ defined by the following table?\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ | $16$ | $25$ |" "What is the domain of the function $f$ defined by the following table?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186088269 :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = t^{2} + t, y = t^{2} - t,-4 \\le t \\le 4,\n$$\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $12$ | $a$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $c$ | $20$ |\n| $y$ | $20$ | $6$ | $2$ | $b$ | $0$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n\nFind the value of $a + b + c$." "For the parametric curve\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = t^{2} + t, y = t^{2} - t,-4 \\le t \\le 4,\n$$\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$t$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$a$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$c$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$b$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFind the value of $a + b + c$."] [:db/cas 17592186088270 :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-4,\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{t}{2},\\,-1\\le t\\le 5,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $a$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $5$ | $12$ | $21$ |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $b$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $c$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a-b+c.$" "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-4,\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{t}{2},\\,-1\\le t\\le 5,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$t$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$a$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{5}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$b$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{3}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$c$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a-b+c.$"] [:db/cas 17592186088858 :tutorial/content "If an object moves at a constant speed for some length of time, we can calculate how far it travels using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{distance} = \\text{speed} \\times \\text{time}\n$$\n\nWe can use this formula to determine how long it takes for two objects to meet.\n\nFor example, suppose a car leaves London for Glasgow, driving at $70$ miles per hour. At the same time, another car departs Glasgow for London, driving at $60$ miles per hour. If the distance between Glasgow and London is $416$ miles, how long until the cars drive past each other?\n\nLet $T$ be the number of hours from when the cars left to when they meet.\n\nWe fill in a speed-time-distance table for each vehicle:\n\n| Car | Speed (mi/hr) | Time (hr) | Distance (mi) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **London to Glasgow** | $70$ | $T$ | $70 T$ |\n| **Glasgow to London** | $60$ | $T$ | $60T$ |\n\nWhen the cars cross paths, they will have driven a combined distance of $416$ miles. Therefore, we can set the sum of the distances equal to $416$ and solve for time, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n70T + 60T &= 416 \\\\\n130T &= 416 \\\\\n\\frac{130T}{130} &= \\frac{416}{130} \\\\\n&= T \\mid \\approx 3.2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cars drive past each other after about $3.2$ hours." "If an object moves at a constant speed for some length of time, we can calculate how far it travels using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{distance} = \\text{speed} \\times \\text{time}\n$$\n\nWe can use this formula to determine how long it takes for two objects to meet.\n\nFor example, suppose a car leaves London for Glasgow, driving at $70$ miles per hour. At the same time, another car departs Glasgow for London, driving at $60$ miles per hour. If the distance between Glasgow and London is $416$ miles, how long until the cars drive past each other?\n\nLet $T$ be the number of hours from when the cars left to when they meet.\n\nWe fill in a speed-time-distance table for each vehicle:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Car</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Speed (mi/hr)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Time (hr)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Distance (mi)</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>London to Glasgow</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$70$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$70 T$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>Glasgow to London</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$60$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$60T$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWhen the cars cross paths, they will have driven a combined distance of $416$ miles. Therefore, we can set the sum of the distances equal to $416$ and solve for time, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n70T + 60T &= 416 \\\\\n130T &= 416 \\\\\n\\frac{130T}{130} &= \\frac{416}{130} \\\\\n&= T \\mid \\approx 3.2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cars drive past each other after about $3.2$ hours."] [:db/cas 17592186088904 :tutorial/content "We can draw a curve by defining a relationship between $x$ and $y$ using a third parameter $t.$ For example, suppose we have \n\n$$\nx=2t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^2, \\quad -3\\leq t\\leq 3.\n$$\n\nThese are the **parametric equations** of the curve.\n\nFirst, let's create a table for some values of $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x= 2t$ | $-6$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| $y=t^2$ | $9$ | $4$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n\nPlotting these points, we get the following curve:\n\n![](images/f7/f716e12c79731f363ace05965809bfc1963a6c3a231e1c4a96eaf3dbb62c848e.png)\n\nThe curve is actually a parabola.\nA curve defined by parametric equations is called a **parametric curve** and is said to be \"defined parametrically\"." "We can draw a curve by defining a relationship between $x$ and $y$ using a third parameter $t.$ For example, suppose we have \n\n$$\nx=2t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^2, \\quad -3\\leq t\\leq 3.\n$$\n\nThese are the **parametric equations** of the curve.\n\nFirst, let's create a table for some values of $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$t$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x= 2t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y=t^2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nPlotting these points, we get the following curve:\n\n![](images/f7/f716e12c79731f363ace05965809bfc1963a6c3a231e1c4a96eaf3dbb62c848e.png)\n\nThe curve is actually a parabola.\nA curve defined by parametric equations is called a **parametric curve** and is said to be \"defined parametrically\"."] [:db/cas 17592186088920 :tutorial/content "**Euler's formula** allows us to convert between complex exponential functions and complex numbers expressed in polar form. It states that\n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm{i}\\theta} = \\cos\\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWith Euler's formula, we can write any complex number $z$ in three equivalent ways. Letting $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\textrm{arg}(z),$ we have the following:\n\n| **** | **Cartesian** | **Polar** | **Exponential** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $x+\\textrm{i}y$ | $r\\left( \\cos\\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta\\right)$ | $re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta}$ |\n\nFor instance, if $z=1+\\textrm{i},$ then we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2}, \\\\ \\theta  & =\\text{arg}(z)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{1}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the complex number $z=1+\\textrm{i}$ can be written in exponential form as\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{2}e^{\\textrm{i}\\pi/4}.\n$$\n\nWhen $\\theta=\\pi,$ we have a special case:\n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm{i}\\pi} = -1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad e^{\\textrm{i}\\pi} +1=0\n$$\n\nThe equation $e^{\\textrm{i}\\pi} +1=0$ is called **Euler's identity**. It is often described as the most beautiful equation in all of mathematics because it combines five important constants $e, \\textrm{i}, \\pi, 1,$ and $0$ into a single elegant form." "**Euler's formula** allows us to convert between complex exponential functions and complex numbers expressed in polar form. It states that\n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm{i}\\theta} = \\cos\\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWith Euler's formula, we can write any complex number $z$ in three equivalent ways. Letting $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\textrm{arg}(z),$ we have the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">****</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Cartesian</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Polar</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Exponential</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$z$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x+\\textrm{i}y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$r\\left( \\cos\\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFor instance, if $z=1+\\textrm{i},$ then we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2}, \\\\ \\theta  & =\\text{arg}(z)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{1}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the complex number $z=1+\\textrm{i}$ can be written in exponential form as\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{2}e^{\\textrm{i}\\pi/4}.\n$$\n\nWhen $\\theta=\\pi,$ we have a special case:\n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm{i}\\pi} = -1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad e^{\\textrm{i}\\pi} +1=0\n$$\n\nThe equation $e^{\\textrm{i}\\pi} +1=0$ is called **Euler's identity**. It is often described as the most beautiful equation in all of mathematics because it combines five important constants $e, \\textrm{i}, \\pi, 1,$ and $0$ into a single elegant form."] [:db/cas 17592186088925 :tutorial/content "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin are\n\n$$\nx=\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\sin\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq \\theta <2\\pi.\n$$\n\nIf we make a table of values and plot them, we can see why this is a circle. Let's label each point $A-H.$\n\n|  | $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,C\\,$ | $\\,D\\,$ | $\\,E\\,$ | $\\,F\\,$ | $\\,G\\,$ | $\\,H\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,\\theta\\,$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ | $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$ | $\\pi$ | $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$ | $\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}$ |\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |\n| $\\,y\\,$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |\n\n![](images/d5/d50cf22b3e7429924ce0039f4daa2191234e628173e6f5be789a872ffa520346.png)\n\nThe $\\theta$ domain $0\\leq \\theta<2\\pi$ ensures that we go around the entire circle. If we were to cut the $\\theta$ domain in half (i.e., $0\\leq\\theta\\leq \\pi$), we would get a semicircle.\n\nFor a general circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin $O,$ the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq \\theta <2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThis parametrization starts at the point $(r,0)$ (corresponding the $\\theta = 0$) and traverses the circle in the counterclockwise direction. This is the most common parameterization of a circle and is the one that we'll mostly use.\n\nAnother set of parametric equations for a circle of radius $r$ centered at $O$ is\n\n$$\nx=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad y=r\\cos\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq \\theta <2\\pi.\n$$" "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin are\n\n$$\nx=\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\sin\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq \\theta <2\\pi.\n$$\n\nIf we make a table of values and plot them, we can see why this is a circle. Let's label each point $A-H.$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,A\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,B\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,C\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,D\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,E\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,F\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,G\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,H\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,\\theta\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\pi$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n![](images/d5/d50cf22b3e7429924ce0039f4daa2191234e628173e6f5be789a872ffa520346.png)\n\nThe $\\theta$ domain $0\\leq \\theta<2\\pi$ ensures that we go around the entire circle. If we were to cut the $\\theta$ domain in half (i.e., $0\\leq\\theta\\leq \\pi$), we would get a semicircle.\n\nFor a general circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin $O,$ the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq \\theta <2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThis parametrization starts at the point $(r,0)$ (corresponding the $\\theta = 0$) and traverses the circle in the counterclockwise direction. This is the most common parameterization of a circle and is the one that we'll mostly use.\n\nAnother set of parametric equations for a circle of radius $r$ centered at $O$ is\n\n$$\nx=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad y=r\\cos\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq \\theta <2\\pi.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088935 :tutorial/content "The **remainder** theorem states that for any polynomial $p(x)$ and number $c,$ when we divide $p(x)$ by $(x-c),$ the remainder is equal to $p(c).$\n\nFor example, consider the polynomial $p(x)=4x^3-5x^2-7x+3$ and the number $c={\\color{red}{2}}.$ First, let's divide $p(x)$ by $(x-{\\color{red}{2}})$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}2$ | $4$ | $-5$ | $-7$ | $3$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $8$ | $6$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $4$ | $3$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n\nWe get a remainder of ${\\color{blue}1}.$ Indeed, when we work out $p({\\color{red}2}),$ we also get a result of ${\\color{blue}1},$ just like the remainder theorem guarantees:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(2) & =4(2)^{3}-5(2)^{2}-7(2)+3 \\\\  & =32-20-14+3 \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo understand why this happens, notice that we can use the result of the synthetic division to rewrite $p(x)$ as\n\n$$\np(x) = (4x^2+3x-1)(x-2)+{\\color{blue}{1}}.\n$$\n\nWhen we substitute $x={\\color{red}{2}}$ into the above, we see that the $(x-2)$ factor turns to $0,$ thereby eliminating the quotient $(4x^2+3x-1)$ and leaving only the remainder of ${\\color{blue}{1}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(2) & =(4\\cdot {2}^{2}+3\\cdot 2-1)(2-2)+1 \\\\  & =(4\\cdot {2}^{2}+3\\cdot 2-1)(0)+1 \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe remainder theorem remains valid even if we substitute $(x-c)$ with $k(x-c),$ as long as $k$  is a non-zero real number. Let's look at an example." "The **remainder** theorem states that for any polynomial $p(x)$ and number $c,$ when we divide $p(x)$ by $(x-c),$ the remainder is equal to $p(c).$\n\nFor example, consider the polynomial $p(x)=4x^3-5x^2-7x+3$ and the number $c={\\color{red}{2}}.$ First, let's divide $p(x)$ by $(x-{\\color{red}{2}})$ using synthetic division:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$-5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$-7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$\\color{gray}\\downarrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$-2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe get a remainder of ${\\color{blue}1}.$ Indeed, when we work out $p({\\color{red}2}),$ we also get a result of ${\\color{blue}1},$ just like the remainder theorem guarantees:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(2) & =4(2)^{3}-5(2)^{2}-7(2)+3 \\\\  & =32-20-14+3 \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo understand why this happens, notice that we can use the result of the synthetic division to rewrite $p(x)$ as\n\n$$\np(x) = (4x^2+3x-1)(x-2)+{\\color{blue}{1}}.\n$$\n\nWhen we substitute $x={\\color{red}{2}}$ into the above, we see that the $(x-2)$ factor turns to $0,$ thereby eliminating the quotient $(4x^2+3x-1)$ and leaving only the remainder of ${\\color{blue}{1}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(2) & =(4\\cdot {2}^{2}+3\\cdot 2-1)(2-2)+1 \\\\  & =(4\\cdot {2}^{2}+3\\cdot 2-1)(0)+1 \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe remainder theorem remains valid even if we substitute $(x-c)$ with $k(x-c),$ as long as $k$  is a non-zero real number. Let's look at an example."] [:db/cas 17592186088995 :tutorial/content "So, we know that the polynomial $p(x) = x^3-2x^2-x+2$ can be factored as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-x+2 & =(x-2)({x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy dividing both sides by $(x-2),$ we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3-2x^2-x+2}{x-2} = x^2-1.\n$$\n\nSince the result of dividing the polynomial $p(x)$ by the factor $({x-{\\color{black}2}})$ gives *another* polynomial, namely $x^2-1,$ we say that $({x-{\\color{black}2}})$ divides $p(x)$ *with no remainder*. \n\nWe can confirm that we get a remainder of zero when $p(x)$ is divided by $({x-{\\color{blue}2}})$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{blue}2$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $2$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $2$ | $0$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ |" "So, we know that the polynomial $p(x) = x^3-2x^2-x+2$ can be factored as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-x+2 & =(x-2)({x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy dividing both sides by $(x-2),$ we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3-2x^2-x+2}{x-2} = x^2-1.\n$$\n\nSince the result of dividing the polynomial $p(x)$ by the factor $({x-{\\color{black}2}})$ gives *another* polynomial, namely $x^2-1,$ we say that $({x-{\\color{black}2}})$ divides $p(x)$ *with no remainder*. \n\nWe can confirm that we get a remainder of zero when $p(x)$ is divided by $({x-{\\color{blue}2}})$ using synthetic division:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{blue}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$\\color{gray}\\downarrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$-2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186091955 :question/problem "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-1.0$ | $-0.7$ | $-0.3$ | $0.7$ | $1.5$ | $2.1$ |\n\nApproximate  at the point $x=3$ using a forward difference approximation." "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1.0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nApproximate  at the point $x=3$ using a forward difference approximation."] [:db/cas 17592186091957 :question/problem "The position of a race car along a straight track, measured in feet from the starting line, is given by the function $s(t),$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $s(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $s(t)$ | $0$ | $3$ | $7$ | $12$ | $19$ |\n\nApproximate the velocity $s'(t)$ at the time $t=0.1$ using a forward difference approximation." "The position of a race car along a straight track, measured in feet from the starting line, is given by the function $s(t),$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $s(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$s(t)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nApproximate the velocity $s'(t)$ at the time $t=0.1$ using a forward difference approximation."] [:db/cas 17592186091959 :question/problem "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $4$ | $7$ | $10$ | $13$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-12$ | $-7$ | $-3$ | $7$ | $15$ | $21$ |\n\nApproximate $f'(x)$ at the point $x=16$ using a backward difference approximation." "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nApproximate $f'(x)$ at the point $x=16$ using a backward difference approximation."] [:db/cas 17592186091961 :question/problem "A race car's position along a straight track, measured in meters from the starting line, is given by the function $s(t),$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $s(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $1.2$ | $1.4$ | $1.6$ | $1.8$ | $2.0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $s(t)$ | $1.3$ | $2.7$ | $2.9$ | $3.5$ | $5.0$ |\n\nApproximate the velocity $s'(t)$ when $t=1.6$ using a backward difference approximation." "A race car's position along a straight track, measured in meters from the starting line, is given by the function $s(t),$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $s(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$s(t)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5.0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nApproximate the velocity $s'(t)$ when $t=1.6$ using a backward difference approximation."] [:db/cas 17592186091962 :question/worked-solution "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{output} = \\text{rate} \\times \\text{time}\n$$\n\nLet $t$ be the number of hours it takes for Agnes and Sheldon to finish the job together.\n\n- Agnes can clean a car in $2$ hours, so she works at a rate of $\\dfrac12$ cars per hour. So, Agnes can clean $\\dfrac{1}{2}t$ cars in $t$ hours.\n- Sheldon can clean a car in $3$ hours, so he works at a rate of $\\dfrac13$ cars per hour. So, Sheldon can clean $\\dfrac{1}{3}t$ cars in $t$ hours.\n\nWe can organize this information in a rate–time–output table:\n\n| Workers | Rate (cars/h) | Time (h) | Output (cars) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Agnes | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $t$ | $\\dfrac{t}{2}$ |\n| Sheldon | $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ | $t$ | $\\dfrac{t}{3}$ |\n\nAgnes and Sheldon together will clean one car. So, the sum of their outputs is $1$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{t}{2} + \\dfrac{t}{3} = 1\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for $t$. To eliminate the fractions, we multiply by the least common multiple of $2$ and $3$, which is $6$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 \\cdot (\\frac{t}{2} + \\frac{t}{3}) &= 6 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n\\frac{6t}{2} + \\frac{6t}{3} &= 6 \\\\\n3t + 2t &= 6 \\\\\n5t &= 6 \\\\\nt &= \\frac{6}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, working together, it takes them\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{5} = 1\\dfrac15\n$$\n\nhours to clean one car." "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{output} = \\text{rate} \\times \\text{time}\n$$\n\nLet $t$ be the number of hours it takes for Agnes and Sheldon to finish the job together.\n\n- Agnes can clean a car in $2$ hours, so she works at a rate of $\\dfrac12$ cars per hour. So, Agnes can clean $\\dfrac{1}{2}t$ cars in $t$ hours.\n- Sheldon can clean a car in $3$ hours, so he works at a rate of $\\dfrac13$ cars per hour. So, Sheldon can clean $\\dfrac{1}{3}t$ cars in $t$ hours.\n\nWe can organize this information in a rate–time–output table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Workers</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Rate (cars/h)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Time (h)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Output (cars)</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Agnes</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{t}{2}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Sheldon</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{t}{3}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nAgnes and Sheldon together will clean one car. So, the sum of their outputs is $1$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{t}{2} + \\dfrac{t}{3} = 1\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for $t$. To eliminate the fractions, we multiply by the least common multiple of $2$ and $3$, which is $6$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 \\cdot (\\frac{t}{2} + \\frac{t}{3}) &= 6 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n\\frac{6t}{2} + \\frac{6t}{3} &= 6 \\\\\n3t + 2t &= 6 \\\\\n5t &= 6 \\\\\nt &= \\frac{6}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, working together, it takes them\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{5} = 1\\dfrac15\n$$\n\nhours to clean one car."] [:db/cas 17592186092002 :question/problem "Use a midpoint Riemann sum with the partition\n$[1,5] = [1,2] \\cup [2,4] \\cup [4,5]$\nto approximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $4.5$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $4$ |" "Use a midpoint Riemann sum with the partition\n$[1,5] = [1,2] \\cup [2,4] \\cup [4,5]$\nto approximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186092003 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/1d/1dcbaa5d87db0d625e62399322ebd21723f1cb30269a8b49a3ad7d43e5f442c8.png)\n\nFirst, let's compute the step size $\\Delta{x}$, which is just the width of each rectangle. In our case, the endpoints of the interval are $a=0.5$ and $b=3.5$, and there are $n=3$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\n\\Delta{x} = \\dfrac{3.5-0.5}{3} = 1\n$$\n\nUsing the midpoints $x=1,2,3$, the area $\\mathcal{A}$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A \\approx (\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(1)}}+{\\color{red}{f(2)}}+{\\color{red}{f(3)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}}) \\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the midpoints of each subinterval:\n\n| **Subinterval** | $x^\\ast$ | $f(x^\\ast)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $[0.5,1.5]$ | $1$ | $12$ |\n| $[1.5,2.5]$ | $2$ | $9$ |\n| $[2.5,3.5]$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\nA \\mid \\approx (12 + 9 + 4) \\cdot 1 = 25\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave down* over the interval $[0.5, 3.5]$, the \"extra\" area between the rectangles and the function is greater than the \"missing\" area, which means our approximation is an *overestimate* of the correct area." "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/1d/1dcbaa5d87db0d625e62399322ebd21723f1cb30269a8b49a3ad7d43e5f442c8.png)\n\nFirst, let's compute the step size $\\Delta{x}$, which is just the width of each rectangle. In our case, the endpoints of the interval are $a=0.5$ and $b=3.5$, and there are $n=3$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\n\\Delta{x} = \\dfrac{3.5-0.5}{3} = 1\n$$\n\nUsing the midpoints $x=1,2,3$, the area $\\mathcal{A}$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A \\approx (\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(1)}}+{\\color{red}{f(2)}}+{\\color{red}{f(3)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}}) \\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the midpoints of each subinterval:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Subinterval</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x^\\ast$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x^\\ast)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$[0.5,1.5]$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$[1.5,2.5]$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$[2.5,3.5]$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\nA \\mid \\approx (12 + 9 + 4) \\cdot 1 = 25\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave down* over the interval $[0.5, 3.5]$, the \"extra\" area between the rectangles and the function is greater than the \"missing\" area, which means our approximation is an *overestimate* of the correct area."] [:db/cas 17592186092004 :question/problem "The following table shows the number of customers per minute that arrive at a counter to be served, measured over a period of $20$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\:\\textrm{(minutes)}$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $13$ | $20$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $C\\:\\textrm{(customers/minute)}$ | $20$ | $30$ | $30$ | $40$ | $35$ |\n\nUse the midpoint Riemann sum with the partitions\n$[0,6],\\quad[6,20]$\nto approximate the number of customers served over the $20$ minute period." "The following table shows the number of customers per minute that arrive at a counter to be served, measured over a period of $20$ minutes.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t\\:\\textrm{(minutes)}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$C\\:\\textrm{(customers/minute)}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$30$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$30$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$40$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$35$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nUse the midpoint Riemann sum with the partitions\n$[0,6],\\quad[6,20]$\nto approximate the number of customers served over the $20$ minute period."] [:db/cas 17592186092004 :question/worked-solution "In this context, we have $n=2$ rectangles and the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the curve $y=C(t)$ represents the total number of customers served over the $20$-minute period.\n\nThe midpoint Riemann sum states that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx C(t_1^\\ast)\\Delta t_1+C(t_2^\\ast)\\Delta t_2\n$$\n\nwhere each $\\Delta t_i$ and $t_i^\\ast$ denote, respectively, the width and the midpoint of the $i$th subinterval.\n\nWe set up a table of values that include the $\\Delta t$'s and the midpoints:\n\n| **Subinterval** | $\\Delta t$ | $t^\\ast$ | $C(t^\\ast)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $[0,6]$ | $6$ | $3$ | $30$ |\n| $[6,20]$ | $14$ | $13$ | $40$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve, and we get\n\n$$\nA \\mid \\approx 30 \\cdot 6 + 40 \\cdot 14; = 180 + 560; = 740\n$$\n\nTherefore, about $740$ customers were served over the $20$-minute period." "In this context, we have $n=2$ rectangles and the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the curve $y=C(t)$ represents the total number of customers served over the $20$-minute period.\n\nThe midpoint Riemann sum states that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx C(t_1^\\ast)\\Delta t_1+C(t_2^\\ast)\\Delta t_2\n$$\n\nwhere each $\\Delta t_i$ and $t_i^\\ast$ denote, respectively, the width and the midpoint of the $i$th subinterval.\n\nWe set up a table of values that include the $\\Delta t$'s and the midpoints:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Subinterval</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t^\\ast$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$C(t^\\ast)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$[0,6]$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$30$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$[6,20]$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$40$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve, and we get\n\n$$\nA \\mid \\approx 30 \\cdot 6 + 40 \\cdot 14; = 180 + 560; = 740\n$$\n\nTherefore, about $740$ customers were served over the $20$-minute period."] [:db/cas 17592186092005 :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)= 18 - x^2$ and let $\\mathcal A$ denote the required area. The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx \\frac{1}{2}\\left[f(a)+ 2\\left(f(x_1) + f(x_2)+\\ldots + f(x_{n-1})\\right)+f(b) \\right]\\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size. \n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=3.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[1,4],$ so we have $a=1$ and $b=4.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n} = \\dfrac{4 - 1}{3} = 1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx \\frac{1}{2}\\left[f(1)+ 2\\left(f(2) + f(3)\\right) + f(4) \\right]\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $x$ and $f(x){:}$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $17$ | $14$ | $9$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\left[17 + 2\\left(14 + 9\\right)+ 2 \\right]\\cdot 1 = \\dfrac{65}{2}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave down* over the interval $[1,4],$ the trapezoids lie under the function, and therefore approximation is an *underestimate* of the correct area." "Let $f(x)= 18 - x^2$ and let $\\mathcal A$ denote the required area. The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx \\frac{1}{2}\\left[f(a)+ 2\\left(f(x_1) + f(x_2)+\\ldots + f(x_{n-1})\\right)+f(b) \\right]\\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size. \n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=3.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[1,4],$ so we have $a=1$ and $b=4.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n} = \\dfrac{4 - 1}{3} = 1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx \\frac{1}{2}\\left[f(1)+ 2\\left(f(2) + f(3)\\right) + f(4) \\right]\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $x$ and $f(x){:}$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td>$1$</td>\n<td>$2$</td>\n<td>$3$</td>\n<td>$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td>$17$</td>\n<td>$14$</td>\n<td>$9$</td>\n<td>$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\left[17 + 2\\left(14 + 9\\right)+ 2 \\right]\\cdot 1 = \\dfrac{65}{2}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave down* over the interval $[1,4],$ the trapezoids lie under the function, and therefore approximation is an *underestimate* of the correct area."] [:db/cas 17592186092006 :question/problem "Using the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[1,4.5] = [1,2] \\cup [2,4] \\cup [4,4.5],\n$$\n\napproximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $4.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |" "Using the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[1,4.5] = [1,2] \\cup [2,4] \\cup [4,4.5],\n$$\n\napproximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td>$1$</td>\n<td>$2$</td>\n<td>$4$</td>\n<td>$4.5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td>$1$</td>\n<td>$5$</td>\n<td>$7$</td>\n<td>$9$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186092007 :question/problem "The following table shows the speed of a car, in miles per hour, at certain moments in its $2$-hour journey.\n\n| $t  \\left(\\textrm{hours}\\right)$ | $0$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v  \\left(\\textrm{miles}/\\textrm{hour}\\right)$ | $0$ | $60$ | $40$ |\n\nUse the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[0,1.5] \\cup [1.5,2],\n$$\n\nto approximate the total distance traveled during the $2$-hour journey." "The following table shows the speed of a car, in miles per hour, at certain moments in its $2$-hour journey.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t  \\left(\\textrm{hours}\\right)$</td>\n<td>$0$</td>\n<td>$1.5$</td>\n<td>$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v  \\left(\\textrm{miles}/\\textrm{hour}\\right)$</td>\n<td>$0$</td>\n<td>$60$</td>\n<td>$40$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nUse the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[0,1.5] \\cup [1.5,2],\n$$\n\nto approximate the total distance traveled during the $2$-hour journey."] [:db/cas 17592186092142 :question/worked-solution "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{output} = \\text{rate} \\times \\text{time}\n$$\n\nLet $t$ be the number of minutes Bryan takes to fill the tire.\n\n- Bryan can fill the tire in $5$ minutes, so he fills at a rate of $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ tires per minute.\n- The leak empties the tire at a rate of $\\dfrac{1}{30}$ tires per minute, which we can think of as filling it at a rate of $-\\dfrac{1}{30}$ tires per minute.\n\nWe can organize this information in a rate-time-output table:\n\n| Work | Rate (tires/min) | Time (min) | Output (tires) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Filling | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ | $t$ | $\\dfrac{t}{5}$ |\n| Leaking | $-\\dfrac{1}{30}$ | $t$ | $-\\dfrac{t}{30}$ |\n\nWorking against the leak, Bryan fills a single tire. So, the sum of the outputs is $1$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{t}{5} - \\dfrac{t}{30} = 1\n$$\n\nWe now solve this for $t$. To eliminate the fractions, we multiply by the least common multiple of $5$ and $30$, which is $30$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n30 \\cdot (\\frac{t}{5} - \\frac{t}{30}) &= 30 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n\\frac{30t}{5} - \\frac{30t}{30} &= 30 \\\\\n6t - t &= 30 \\\\\n5t &= 30 \\\\\nt &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Bryan can fill the tire in $6$ minutes using the faulty pump." "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{output} = \\text{rate} \\times \\text{time}\n$$\n\nLet $t$ be the number of minutes Bryan takes to fill the tire.\n\n- Bryan can fill the tire in $5$ minutes, so he fills at a rate of $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ tires per minute.\n- The leak empties the tire at a rate of $\\dfrac{1}{30}$ tires per minute, which we can think of as filling it at a rate of $-\\dfrac{1}{30}$ tires per minute.\n\nWe can organize this information in a rate-time-output table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Work</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Rate (tires/min)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Time (min)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Output (tires)</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Filling</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{5}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{t}{5}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Leaking</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{1}{30}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{t}{30}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWorking against the leak, Bryan fills a single tire. So, the sum of the outputs is $1$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{t}{5} - \\dfrac{t}{30} = 1\n$$\n\nWe now solve this for $t$. To eliminate the fractions, we multiply by the least common multiple of $5$ and $30$, which is $30$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n30 \\cdot (\\frac{t}{5} - \\frac{t}{30}) &= 30 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n\\frac{30t}{5} - \\frac{30t}{30} &= 30 \\\\\n6t - t &= 30 \\\\\n5t &= 30 \\\\\nt &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Bryan can fill the tire in $6$ minutes using the faulty pump."] [:db/cas 17592186092143 :question/worked-solution "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part} = \\textrm{percentage} \\times \\textrm{total}\n$$\n\nLet $x$ be the number of milliliters of the $2\\%$ acid solution that must be added to the $300$ milliliters of $7\\%$ acid solution. Then there will be a total of $300+x$ milliliters of the resulting $5\\%$ acid solution.\n\nWe organize this information in a percentage–total–part table:\n\n| % Acid | Total (ml) | Part Acid (ml) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $7$ | $300$ | $0.07 \\cdot 300$ |\n| $2$ | $x$ | $0.02 \\cdot x$ |\n| $5$ | $300+x$ | $0.05 \\cdot (300+x)$ |\n\nThe parts acid in each of the $7\\%$ and $2\\%$ solutions add up to the part acid in the $5\\%$ solution. Therefore,\n\n$$\n0.07 \\cdot 300 + 0.02 \\cdot x = 0.05 \\cdot (300+x)\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.07 \\cdot 300 + 0.02 \\cdot x &= 0.05 \\cdot (300 + x) \\\\\n21 + 0.02x &= 15 + 0.05x \\\\\n6 + 0.02x &= 0.05x \\\\\n6 &= 0.03x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $100$ allows us to solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n100 \\cdot 6 &= 100 \\cdot 0.03x \\\\\n600 &= 3x \\\\\n200 &= x \\\\\nx &= 200\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $200$ milliliters of the $2\\%$ solution must be added." "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part} = \\textrm{percentage} \\times \\textrm{total}\n$$\n\nLet $x$ be the number of milliliters of the $2\\%$ acid solution that must be added to the $300$ milliliters of $7\\%$ acid solution. Then there will be a total of $300+x$ milliliters of the resulting $5\\%$ acid solution.\n\nWe organize this information in a percentage–total–part table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">% Acid</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Total (ml)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Part Acid (ml)</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$300$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.07 \\cdot 300$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.02 \\cdot x$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$300+x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$0.05 \\cdot (300+x)$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe parts acid in each of the $7\\%$ and $2\\%$ solutions add up to the part acid in the $5\\%$ solution. Therefore,\n\n$$\n0.07 \\cdot 300 + 0.02 \\cdot x = 0.05 \\cdot (300+x)\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.07 \\cdot 300 + 0.02 \\cdot x &= 0.05 \\cdot (300 + x) \\\\\n21 + 0.02x &= 15 + 0.05x \\\\\n6 + 0.02x &= 0.05x \\\\\n6 &= 0.03x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $100$ allows us to solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n100 \\cdot 6 &= 100 \\cdot 0.03x \\\\\n600 &= 3x \\\\\n200 &= x \\\\\nx &= 200\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $200$ milliliters of the $2\\%$ solution must be added."] [:db/cas 17592186092144 :question/worked-solution "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part} = \\textrm{percentage} \\times \\textrm{total}\n$$\n\nSince the ratio of the two concentrations has to be the same in any amount of the $50\\%$ concentrated mixture, let's assume we wish to make $100$ gallons of this mixture.\n\nLet $x$ be the number of gallons of the $20\\%$ gray paint used. Since the total mixture must be $100$ gallons, the rest of the mixture will consist of $100-x$ gallons of the $60\\%$ gray paint.\n\nWe organize this information in a percentage–total–part table:\n\n| % Gray | Total (gal) | Part Gray (gal) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $20$ | $x$ | $0.2 \\cdot x$ |\n| $60$ | $100-x$ | $0.6 \\cdot (100-x)$ |\n| $50$ | $100$ | $0.5 \\cdot 100$ |\n\nThe parts gray in each of the $20\\%$ and $60\\%$ paints add up to the part gray in the $50\\%$ paint:\n\n$$\n0.2 \\cdot x + 0.6 \\cdot (100-x) = 0.5 \\cdot 100\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.2 \\cdot x + 0.6 \\cdot (100 - x) &= 0.5 \\cdot 100 \\\\\n0.2x + 60 - 0.6x &= 50 \\\\\n- 0.4x &= -10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $10$ allows us to solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10 \\cdot (-0.4x) &= 10 \\cdot (-10) \\\\\n- 4x &= -100 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{-100}{-4} \\\\\nx &= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $25$ gallons of the $20\\%$ gray paint must be mixed with $100-25=75$ gallons of the $60\\%$ gray paint.\n\nThe required ratio of the $20\\%$ concentration to the $60\\%$ concentration is therefore $25:75$, or $1:3$." "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part} = \\textrm{percentage} \\times \\textrm{total}\n$$\n\nSince the ratio of the two concentrations has to be the same in any amount of the $50\\%$ concentrated mixture, let's assume we wish to make $100$ gallons of this mixture.\n\nLet $x$ be the number of gallons of the $20\\%$ gray paint used. Since the total mixture must be $100$ gallons, the rest of the mixture will consist of $100-x$ gallons of the $60\\%$ gray paint.\n\nWe organize this information in a percentage–total–part table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">% Gray</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Total (gal)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Part Gray (gal)</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2 \\cdot x$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$60$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$100-x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.6 \\cdot (100-x)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$50$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$0.5 \\cdot 100$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe parts gray in each of the $20\\%$ and $60\\%$ paints add up to the part gray in the $50\\%$ paint:\n\n$$\n0.2 \\cdot x + 0.6 \\cdot (100-x) = 0.5 \\cdot 100\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.2 \\cdot x + 0.6 \\cdot (100 - x) &= 0.5 \\cdot 100 \\\\\n0.2x + 60 - 0.6x &= 50 \\\\\n- 0.4x &= -10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $10$ allows us to solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10 \\cdot (-0.4x) &= 10 \\cdot (-10) \\\\\n- 4x &= -100 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{-100}{-4} \\\\\nx &= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $25$ gallons of the $20\\%$ gray paint must be mixed with $100-25=75$ gallons of the $60\\%$ gray paint.\n\nThe required ratio of the $20\\%$ concentration to the $60\\%$ concentration is therefore $25:75$, or $1:3$."] [:db/cas 17592186092580 :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}.$ Calculate $P(Y\\ge 1).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ | $\\,0.8264\\,$ | $\\,0.8289\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ | $\\,0.8508\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ |" "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}.$ Calculate $P(Y\\ge 1).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.03\\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.8\\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.7967\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.7995\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.8023\\,$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.9\\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.8238\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.8264\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.8289\\,$</td></tr>\n<tr> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.0\\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.8485\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.8508\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.8531\\,$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186092581 :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}-{X}_{3}.$ Find $P(Y<1).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ | $\\,0.7190\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ | $\\,0.7454\\,$ | $\\,0.7486\\,$ | $\\,0.7517\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ | $\\,0.7794\\,$ | $\\,0.7823\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ | $\\,0.8051\\,$ | $\\,0.8078\\,$ | $\\,0.8106\\,$ |" "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}-{X}_{3}.$ Find $P(Y<1).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.5\\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.7088\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.7123\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.7157\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.7190\\,$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.6\\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.7422\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.7454\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.7486\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.7517\\,$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.7\\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.7734\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.7764\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.7794\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.7823\\,$</td></tr>\n<tr> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.8\\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.8023\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.8051\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.8078\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.8106\\,$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186092619 :question/problem "Use a midpoint Riemann sum with the partition\n$[- 2, 3] = [- 2, 0]∪[0, 1]∪[1, 2]∪[2, 3]$\nto approximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y = f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $2.5$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1.25$ | $2$ | $3.25$ | $5$ | $7.25$ | $10$ |\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." "Use a midpoint Riemann sum with the partition\n$[- 2, 3] = [- 2, 0]∪[0, 1]∪[1, 2]∪[2, 3]$\nto approximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y = f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7.25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186092632 :question/problem "Use a midpoint Riemann sum with the partition\n$[0, 30] = [0, 14]∪[14, 30]$\nto approximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y = f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $7$ | $9$ | $14$ | $17$ | $22$ | $25$ | $30$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $9$ | $15$ | $20$ | $40$ | $67$ | $80$ | $91$ | $95$ | $99$ |\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." "Use a midpoint Riemann sum with the partition\n$[0, 30] = [0, 14]∪[14, 30]$\nto approximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y = f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$30$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$40$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$67$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$80$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$91$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$95$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$99$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186092633 :question/problem "The continuous function $P(t)$ shows the rate at which people walk into a store at time $t$ hours. There were $15$ people present in the store at time $t = 0$. The values for $P(t)$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $t(hours)$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $7$ | $10$ | $13$ | $16$ | $18$ | $20$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(people/hour)$ | $15$ | $10$ | $7$ | $15$ | $20$ | $35$ | $45$ | $50$ | $60$ |\n\nUse the midpoint Riemann sum with the partitions\n$[0, 4], [4, 10], [10, 16], [16, 20]$\nto approximate the total number of people that have walked into the store by time $t = 20$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." "The continuous function $P(t)$ shows the rate at which people walk into a store at time $t$ hours. There were $15$ people present in the store at time $t = 0$. The values for $P(t)$ are given in the ta