n. The values of $n(t)$, in thousands, for different values of $t$ are given in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$n(t)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$851$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$856$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$861$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$865$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$870$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nApproximate the rate of growth in population in the second year $(t=2)$ using a forward difference approximation."] [:db/cas 17592186092717 :question/problem "The function $g(s)$ describes the amount of fuel, in liters, remaining in Samuel's car after driving $s$ miles. The values of $g(s)$ for different values of $s$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $s$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(s)$ | $15$ | $13.5$ | $12.1$ | $9.4$ | $8.2$ | $6.6$ | $4.8$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change in the fuel volume when $s=8$ miles using a forward difference approximation." "The function $g(s)$ describes the amount of fuel, in liters, remaining in Samuel's car after driving $s$ miles. The values of $g(s)$ for different values of $s$ are given in the table below:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$s$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g(s)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9.4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6.6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4.8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nApproximate the rate of change in the fuel volume when $s=8$ miles using a forward difference approximation."] [:db/cas 17592186092727 :question/problem "The altitude of an apple that is dropped from the top of a building, measured in meters, is given by the function $s(t)$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $s(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $s(t)$ | $417$ | $412$ | $397$ | $373$ | $339$ |\n\nApproximate the velocity $s^{′}(t)$ when $t = 2$ using a backward difference approximation.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." "The altitude of an apple that is dropped from the top of a building, measured in meters, is given by the function $s(t)$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $s(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</t